<tr><td>CRPT011279</td><td>F000658</td><td>Holsum Bakery</td><td>Minor</td><td>Bakery</td><td>CMP004372</td><td>Holsum Bakery of Tolleson LLC</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Bread Oven Oxidizer Downtime Failure. 

11/22 - 5:30 -8:16pm - Bread Oxidizer - Water in Motor.  

During the investigation, the Engineering team found two separate issues for the root cause of the oxidizer failure. 
Tolleson Engineering Team inspected the system.  Water was found inside the electrical enclosure located on the feed line to the Combustion Fan motor, as well as moisture inside the motor junction box. This caused: Loss of electrical insulation, intermittent ground leakage, Activation of the VFD protection with fault F013-Ground Fault, and subsequent loss of differential pressure in the oxidizer system.  
The affected wires and connections were cleaned and dried by the Engineering Team.   The grounding connection inside the box was tightened. The same drying procedure was performed inside the motor terminal box.  


11/22 -11:43pm – 11/23 8:14am - Bread Oxidizer - Tripping control breaker.

A later issue was identified which was a frayed wire in the conduit for a pressure switch as well as water damage.  Tolleson Engineering team ran a new wire to fix this issue.    As for a preventative measure Tolleson will contact 3rd party contractors regarding running all new wire on the opposite side that was recently completed.  The system is functioning as normal currently.
11/23 8:16am
The bread oxidizer system was restarted and resumed normal operation without further faults.

As part of the investigation, two separate issues were identified causing the oxidizer failure. 

Root Cause:  

1.	Water was found inside the electrical enclosure located on the feed line to the Combustion Fan motor, as well as moisture inside the motor junction box. This caused: Loss of electrical insulation, intermittent ground leakage, Activation of the VFD protection with fault F013-Ground Fault, and subsequent loss of differential pressure in the oxidizer system.  

2.	Frayed wire in the conduit for a pressure switch as well as water damage.  

Corrective Actions :
1. The affected wires and connections were cleaned and dried by the Engineering Team.   The grounding connection inside the box was tightened. The same drying procedure was performed inside the motor terminal box.
2.  Tolleson Engineering team ran a new wire to fix this issue.    

As for a preventative measure Tolleson will contact 3rd party contractors regarding running all new wire on the opposite side that was recently completed

 
During the time of maintenance the following products were ran through the oven without the oxidizer running:

Hoeny Wheat  112 sacks , 14504 Units. 				
				
PL Round Top Whate  78 Sacks, 9594 Units				
				
GV White 130 Sacks, 16290 Units				
				
GV Wheat 52 Sacks,  6976 units				
				
24 OZ Wonder  52 sacks, 5580 units				
				
24 oz White Box Bread  260 units, 2720 units.</td><td>11/25/2025</td><td>12/03/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>This Compliance Report was submitted due to equipment failure. The bread oven oxidizer failed, and the following permit conditions were met regarding this event:

Permit Condition 1.a: 12-month rolling totals of VOCs remained below the 45,100 lbs/yr, with a value of 13,299.8398 lbs/yr (see 12 Month Rolling Totals attachment).

Permit Condition 15: The malfunction was documented in a manner compliant to Rules 130 and 140. 

NOTE:
If additional failures happen in the future, a more specific timeline regarding the corrective action(s) is preferred.</td></tr>
