<tr><td>CRPT011098</td><td>F001759</td><td>Gila River Power Station</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP015964</td><td>Salt River Project</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Title V Compliance Certification</td><td>Enclosed, please find the Semi-Annual Compliance Certification and Monitoring Report & Part 60 Excess Emissions and Monitoring Systems Performance Report for Salt River Project Agricultural Improvement and Power District (SRP) Gila River Power Station (Gila River), reflecting compliance status from April 1, 2025, to September 30, 2025, as required by Permit Condition 21.a. of the Gila River Title V Permit P0009823. A single compliance certification is included in Attachment 1 to demonstrate compliance. Additionally, enclosed is the semi-annual monitoring report, summarizing monitoring results as required by the specific conditions of the Gila River Title V Permit in effect for this monitoring period. The monitoring summaries coincide with the compliance certification period and reflect data and observations from April 1, 2025, to September 30, 2025.</td><td>10/28/2025</td><td>10/29/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>The compliance report for the period April 1, 2025 - September 30, 2025, was submitted within the appropriate time frame.

The report includes a signed certification statement by the Responsible Official. 

The report includes, at a minimum, the following:

The report summarized the compliance status with respect to each permit contained in the permit.

The report demonstrates this requirement by describing and explaining any deviations from any permit condition at any time. 

The facility denotes deviations from various permit conditions as follows:

Permit Condition 18.a.
An excess emissions event occurred at the facility on April 24, 2025. Unit 1A exceeded the 3-hour rolling average NOx concentration limit in Permit Condition 18.a.iv. Table 5 of 2.5 ppm corrected to 15% O2. 

An email was sent to MCAQD on April 25, 2025. The email served as the 24-hour notification of an excess emission event. Discovery of the event occurred on April 24, 2025. 

A 72-hour compliance report (CRPT010160) was submitted to MCAQD dated April 28, 2025.

More information regarding the letter and notice of non-compliance that was documented can be referenced in inspection INSP208936.

For future preventative measures and corrective action, SRP has put in place a more rigorous review procedure with the combustion hardware manufacturer which requires SRP
approval before implementing any changes to the AutoTune software.

The report includes the 12-month rolling total hours of startup and shutdown operation and monthly normal and duct burner operating hours for each combined cycle.

No visible emissions were observed on site during the reporting period.

No odor complaints were received during the reporting period.

The emergency fire pump is fueled with ultra-low sulfur diesel fuel. Bill of lading shows the facility receives ultralow sulfur diesel fuel.

The summary of the monthly and 12-month rolling total records of gasoline is included in the report.
12-month rolling throughput of gasoline: 2,523 gallons

The gasoline AST at the facility meets the definition of insignificant activity as defined in MCAQD Rule 100 Section 200.61.k, thus no inspections are conducted. 

The monthly and 12-month rolling total emissions from the Mechanical Spray Evaporators (MSEs) are included in in the compliance report. 
12-month rolling total PM: 0.0 tons (Permit Limit: 15.1 tons)
12-month rolling total PM10: 0.0 tons (Permit Limit: 7.0 tons)
12-month rolling total PM2.5: 0.0 tons (Permit Limit: 4.1 tons)

The MSEs were out of service from April , 2025, through September 30, 2025, therefore no wind speed records are required during this reporting period.

The performance summary reports for the CEMS analyzers is included in the report.

SRP has prepared a Part 60 Excess Emissions and Monitoring System Performance Report to satisfy the reporting requirements of the appliable subparts of 40 CFR Part 60 as specified by the Permit conditions 21.d. This Part 60 Report covers the reporting period from April 1 2025 - September 30, 2025.

During the reporting period, Gila River updated the high-range NOx analyzer span and range from 0 -100 ppm to 0 - 200 ppm across all combined cycle units to align with SRP's fleet-wide
standards. This change also accommodates NOx concentrations that are briefly observed during transition from start-up mode into steady state that may have exceeded the original 0 -
100 ppm span and range.

There were no instances of excess emissions of 1 percent or more of total operating time or CEMS downtime of 5 percent or more of the total operating time.

No compliance issues were noted during the review of the compliance report.</td></tr>
