<tr><td>CRPT011016</td><td>F000789</td><td>Microchip Technology - Tempe</td><td>Minor</td><td>Semiconductors and Electronics</td><td>CMP002193</td><td>Microchip Technology Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>On October 12, 2025, from approximately 2:15 PM to 3:25 PM and 3:45 PM tot 4:45 PM, the Munters VOC Abatement Unit RCTO 20-2 was offline due to “combustion actuator fault".  We are not currently running any manufacturing processes which emit VOC's due to announce plant closure.”</td><td>10/14/2025</td><td>10/27/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>On Monday, October 27, 2025; I spoke to Terry McNally on the phone and he stated the facility is not in production or emitting any VOC's.  Mr. McNally also stated the RTO has been shut down after email from MCAQD Inspector Supervisor, LeShawn Geter on Monday, October 13, 2025. I attached a copy of the email to following compliance report CRPT0011018 for reference.

The provided summary states the RCTO-20-2 was offline due to two separate faults and identified a "combustion actuator fault", The submitted report describes a "Rotor Rotation Fault". I spoke to Mr. McNally on the phone for clarification and he stated the two malfunctions reported were both Rotor Rotation Faults. The description in the online portal submission was errata.

This appears compliant with Permit Condition 6.a. which is contingent upon the RTO controlling the emissions of VOCs. If there are no VOC's to control, the RTO would not be required to be on.

The facility's reporting indicates no violations to the current Air Quality Permit.</td></tr>
