<tr><td>CRPT010823</td><td>F000789</td><td>Microchip Technology - Tempe</td><td>Minor</td><td>Semiconductors and Electronics</td><td>CMP002193</td><td>Microchip Technology Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>On August 30th, 2025 from 7:24 AM to 9:11 AM, the Munters VOC Abatement Unit RCTO 20-2 was offline due to “VFD (Variable Frequency Drive) over temperature fault”.  We currently are not running any manufacturing processes which emit VOC’s due to announced plant closure.</td><td>09/02/2025</td><td>09/23/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>The facility reports that on August 30, 2025 from 7:24 a.m. to 9:11 a.m. the RCTO 20-2 went offline for 1.75 hours  due to “VFD Variable Frequency Drive” following which the unit resumed operation. The facility calculates the potential excess emissions at 8.925 lbs; but notes the facility is not currently running any manufacturing process which emits VOC's on account the facility is is being closed and resold as previously announced.

Per section 6.a.iii of the facility's permit [Permit ID: P0011578], a period of up to 4-hours bypass period may be allowed in order to start up the unit acting as the backup VOC abatement unit, and all excess VOC emissions shall be calculated without consideration to emissions reductions.

I telephoned Terry McNally about the alleged status of manufacturing processes. Mr. McNally explained the public announcement was on stock forums and Channel 3 and 5. No correspondence was submitted to MCAQD about the sale. Mr. McNally also clarified statement, "We currently are not running any manufacturing processes which emit VOC’s." to mean, the facility is currently not running any manufacturing processes. Period.

Mr. McNally adds the resale of the facility is almost imminent but the details are not yet possible to announce.

The facility's reporting indicates no violations to their Air Quality Permit.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT010822</td><td>F000658</td><td>Holsum Bakery</td><td>Minor</td><td>Bakery</td><td>CMP004372</td><td>Holsum Bakery of Tolleson LLC</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Holsum Bakery F000658 Oxidizer downtime for the Bread Production Line on 9-1-25. The Bread Oven ran from 3:15am – 7:35am without the oxidizer running (4 Hours and 20 Mins, roughly).  Engineering Preflight check was completed by an Engineer at midnight, and all systems were functioning normally.   A breaker tripped in the Bread Oxidizer Oven Panel (unknown time).  At 7:35 an Engineer observed the issue and reset the breaker.  All systems function normally after the reset. Engineering is investigating the failure and why the alarm did not sound with the failure.  


Perfectly Crafted White – started at 2:15 am at divider, went through oven around 3:15am – 10 doughs, approximately 16,240 units
 
 Wonder Italian – started at 5:52 am at divider, went through oven around 6:52 am- 1dough, approximately 1,200 units
 
 Perfectly crafted Multi Grain– started at 6:28 am at divider, went through oven around 7:28am – 11 doughs approximately 17,820 units
 
100% Wheat – started at 8:52 am at divider, went through oven 9:52 am – 1 dough, approximately 1,300 units
 
Perfectly Crafted White – 140 Sacks - approximately 16,240 units
Wonder Italian – 10 Sacks - approximately 1,200 units
Perfectly Crafted MG – 132 - Sacks approximately 17,820 units
100% Wheat – 10 Sacks- approximately 1,300 units</td><td>09/01/2025</td><td>10/07/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Per permit condition 9:

In the event that essential control system maintenance is required (either as a result of a preventive maintenance schedule or an equipment malfunction), bypass is permitted if it can be demonstrated that bakery VOC emissions from new or existing oven can be reduced by at least 81% over the calendar month in which the maintenance occurs (or use a VOC emission factor value of 5.5 pounds per ton for new ovens while the maintenance occurs) and the monthly emission limit is not exceeded. For the purpose of this demonstration, emissions will be calculated using production data, emission factors derived from Rule 343, and the average control system treatment efficiency obtained during the most recent Performance Test.  

The Permittee shall notify the Control Officer of the bypass operation within 24 hours of the occurrence and provide an estimate of the duration of such operation.  

Bypass activities must be appropriately documented at the facility and these records shall be available for review during facility inspections conducted by Department personnel.  

Bread production oxidizer malfunction occurred on 09/01/25, notification submitted to the Department on 09/01/25. The malfunction notification and documentation has been accepted.

Permit expiration: 03/31/29
Annual administrative fees: 02/08/25
Performance testing: Conducted 06/24/25, report submitted 08/07/25
Routine inspection: 05/30/24, in compliance</td></tr>
