<tr><td>CRPT010434</td><td>F000763</td><td>Arizona State University Tempe Campus</td><td>Synthetic Minor</td><td>Institutions - Public/Government</td><td>CMP000336</td><td>Arizona State University</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Dear Ms. Valenzula,
Arizona State University (ASU) is notifying the Maricopa County Air Quality Department (MCAQD) of an event that occurred at the Tempe Campus, Facility ID No. F000763.  ASU conducted a thorough investigation and does not believe that the event caused a deviation/violation. However, in the spirit of transparency, ASU would like to notify the department of the concern. 
The Novus Parking Structure generator is a new generator that has been installed under a minor modification submitted on January 12, 2024, which is currently being processed by the department’s Permitting Division. In September 2024, the generator hours for the Novus Parking Structure generator documented an increase of 145.9 hours. 
Timeline of events: 
•	June 17, 2024, ASU Facility Maintenance attempted to operate the generator and noted that it would “crank” but not start.  
•	The fuel level on June 17, 2024, was documented as full in the ASU work order.  On the same day, a purchase order was created for our vendor to evaluate and fix the generator.  
•	The vendor documented the cause of the generator failure as contaminated fuel.  
•	The generator did not operate until our vendor, Empire CAT, removed the contaminated fuel and replaced it with new fuel.  
•	September 2024, a total of 750 gallons of contaminated fuel was removed, and 800 gallons of new fuel was delivered.  
•	October 2024, ASU’s October monthly work order indicates that the fuel tank was full. 
Generator Facts:
•	The generator consumes 11.6 gallons per hour at ¼ load on standby. As shown by the manufacturer specifications. 
•	If the generator operated at ¼ load (or greater) for 145.9 hours, then about 1,692 gallons of fuel would have been consumed, which is double the tank capacity and would have required refueling during these operating hours. 
•	The only refueling that occurred was in September 2024, when Empire CAT removed the contaminated fuel and added the new fuel to the generator. 
ASU has thoroughly investigated and reviewed the situation.  We interviewed our Facility Maintenance group and corresponded with Empire CAT.  We cannot officially document the reason for the generator hours, but the fuel consumption data indicates the generator did not operate 145.9 hours. To prevent a recurrence, ASU is now leveraging power-outage data and notification from APS to ensure the generator only runs during actual outages. 
If you have any questions or would like to meet to discuss the situation, you are more than welcome to contact me.

Regards,
Tom Ekren
Environmental Programs Manager
Environmental, Health, and Safety
Arizona State University
(480) 788-7278</td><td>06/27/2025</td><td>07/15/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>An initial review of the records provided by the facility was conducted to assess compliance with applicable regulations. Based on the preliminary information, no violations were identified. However, it was determined that additional documentation was necessary to fully support the facility's claims.

A formal request for records was submitted to the facility on July 15, 2025. In response, the facility provided supplemental documentation on July 25, 2025, including the following:

1. Maintenance Records (June 17 and 26, 2024): These records indicate that the engine failed to start on both dates. Notations confirm that the engine’s fuel tank was full at the time.

2. Vendor Work Order (Empire CAT, September 30, 2024): This document details the removal of 750 gallons of "contaminated" diesel fuel from the engine’s tank and its replacement with 800 gallons of clean diesel fuel.

3. Email from ASU Electrical Technician: The technician confirmed that an inspection of the run-time meter revealed no stored or active faults, errors, or malfunctions in either the control panel or the meter itself. The technician concluded that the issue had been resolved and was not expected to reoccur.

Based on the information contained in these records, it has been determined that the facility is in full compliance with all applicable regulations.</td></tr>
