<tr><td>CRPT010380</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Bryan, Brandon, Frank, Marshall, Aaron (and Scott)  – 

The Arizona Department of Environmental Quality website indicates a high pollution advisory with a restricted burn period has been declared for tomorrow 6/18.  There is a test under discussion which is scheduled to run tomorrow which falls under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing of commercial products:  Section 321, subsections 7 & 8.  
 
The planned schedule for this series of tests is stress testing a small format lithium-ion batteries weighing under 1 pound which, if it catches fire during the testing, would, combined with all other testing scheduled for tomorrow, emit less than the 2 lbs allowed during a restricted burn period under those rules.  We used a calculated emissions factor from peer reviewed publications (attached).  This emission factor is more conservative and less permissive than AP42: Open Burning - Vehicle Parts by a factor of two (dependent upon testing).  

This specific proposed test (A):
Batteries initial mass per day for this testing series:  1 lb 
High end of historical mass loss (e.g. fuel): 40%
Emission factor: 11.1%
(initial mass)*(mass loss %)*(emission factor) = 0.044 pounds of particulate matter 

Other Testing Scheduled for tomorrow under separate approval request (B):
Batteries initial mass per day (potential):  16.8 lbs 
High end of historical mass loss (e.g. fuel): 40%
Emission factor: 11.1%
(initial mass)*(mass loss %)*(emission factor) = 0.746 pounds of particulate matter 

Combined Testing Emissions for tomorrow
Particulate Emissions from test (A) + Particulate Emissions from test (B) = 0.044 + 0.746 = 0.790 pounds of particulate matter cumulative for our site</td><td>06/17/2025</td><td>06/23/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Formal submittal for controlled burning/testing on no burn days.

Email was received on June 17 for testing to be conducted on June 18-24. It was approved by Marshall Clark as PM are indicated to be under 2 lbs./day.

Approval email was sent on June 18, 2025.

Sites total particulate matter is as follows.
Combined Testing Emissions for tomorrow June 18, 2025.
Particulate Emissions from test (A) + Particulate Emissions from test (B) = 0.044 + 0.746 = 0.790 pounds of particulate matter cumulative for our site</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT010379</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Bryan, Brandon, Frank, Marshall, Aaron, (and Scott) – 

We have been requested to continue this previously approved testing (below) and are seeking an additional authorization from tomorrow and through till June 24th.  Note, a high pollution advisory has been declared for tomorrow. 

--Begin Forwarded/Repeated Message--

The Arizona Department of Environmental Quality website indicates a high pollution advisory with a restricted burn period has been declared for today and tomorrow.  Should weather patterns hold, it will potentially extend through parts of the next two weeks.  There is a series of tests in discussion which would occur periodically over the next two weeks, including today and tomorrow, which falls under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing of commercial products:  Section 321, subsections 7 & 8.  
 
The planned schedule for each applicable day is stress testing up to three small format lithium-ion batteries weighing 5.6 pounds each which, if they all catch fire during the testing, would emit less than the 2 lbs allowed during a restricted burn period under those rules.  We used a calculated emissions factor from peer reviewed publications (attached).  This emission factor is more conservative and less permissive than AP42: Open Burning - Vehicle Parts by a factor of two (dependent upon testing).  
 
Batteries initial mass per day (potential):  16.8 lbs 
High end of historical mass loss (e.g. fuel): 40%
Emission factor: 11.1%
(initial mass)*(mass loss %)*(emission factor) = 0.746 pounds of particulate matter</td><td>06/17/2025</td><td>06/23/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Formal submittal for controlled burning/testing on no burn days.

Email was received on June 17 for testing to be conducted on June 18-24. It was approved by Marshall Clark as PM are indicated to be under 2 lbs./day.

Approval email was sent on June 18, 2025.

Sites total particulate matter is as follows.
Combined Testing Emissions for tomorrow June 18, 2025.
Particulate Emissions from test (A) + Particulate Emissions from test (B) = 0.044 + 0.746 = 0.790 pounds of particulate matter cumulative for our site</td></tr>
