<tr><td>CRPT010216</td><td>F002681</td><td>SFPP-Phoenix Terminal</td><td>Title V</td><td>Petroleum Products Bulk Storage/Handling</td><td>CMP002921</td><td>SFPP LP</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>SVE April 2025 Monthly Sampling Report</td><td>05/08/2025</td><td>05/20/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>SVE sampling report for April 2025. The compliance report covers the requirements of Permit Condition 34.a/b/d. The report was submitted in a timely manner on 5/08/2025, with notes as follows:

SVE UNIT 1

- 34.d.i) Sampling completed per graduated schedule: initial, 4 biweekly, 6 monthly
-- Analysis performed by Eurofins
- 34.d.ii) Flow rate: 152scfm (limit: 300scfm)
- 34.d.iii) Process temperature: 745F (minimum limit: 626F)
- 34.d.iv) Total concentration of halogenated compounds at inlet: 0.03ppmv (limit: 2,200ppmv)
- 34.d.v) Total concentration of VOC at inlet: 8ppmv (limit: 2,200ppmv)
- 34.d.vi) VOC destruction efficiency: 100% (minimum limit: 98%)

SVE UNIT 2

- 34.d.i) Sampling completed per graduated schedule: initial, 4 biweekly, 6 monthly
-- Analysis performed by Eurofins
- 34.d.ii) Flow rate: 130scfm (limit: 300scfm)
- 34.d.iii) Process temperature: 786F (minimum limit: 626F)
- 34.d.iv) Total concentration of halogenated compounds at inlet: 0.14ppmv (limit: 2,200ppmv)
- 34.d.v) Total concentration of VOC at inlet: 280ppmv (limit: 2,200ppmv)
- 34.d.vi) VOC destruction efficiency: 99% (minimum limit: 98%)

SVE UNIT 3

- 34.d.i) Sampling completed per graduated schedule: initial, 6 biweekly, 3 monthly
-- Analysis performed by Eurofins
- 34.d.ii) Flow rate: 80scfm (limit: 300scfm)
- 34.d.iii) Process temperature: 729F (minimum limit: 626F)
- 34.d.iv) Total concentration of halogenated compounds at inlet: 0.04ppmv (limit: 2,200ppmv)
- 34.d.v) Total concentration of VOC at inlet: 1.0ppmv (limit: 2,200ppmv)
- 34.d.vi) VOC destruction efficiency: 100% (minimum limit: 98%)

No issues of non-compliance determined during review of the report. See INSP209413 for further details.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT010224</td><td>F050885</td><td>PCA Phoenix/Glendale</td><td>Minor</td><td>Pulp and Paper Plant</td><td>CMP002402</td><td>Packaging Corporation of America</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>O&M Plan</td><td>Updated O&M Plan for Starch Silo Operations</td><td>05/08/2025</td><td>05/16/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>O&M plan accepted</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT010223</td><td>F001486</td><td>Packaging Corporation Of America</td><td>Minor</td><td>Pulp and Paper Plant</td><td>CMP002402</td><td>Packaging Corporation of America</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Permit Close Request</td><td>Permanent closure of plant and permit cancellation request.  Operations have been relocated to another facility under a different Facility ID and permit.</td><td>05/08/2025</td><td>05/15/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT010220</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>The Arizona Department of Environmental Quality website indicates a high pollution advisory with a restricted burn period has been declared for tomorrow and a high pollution warning has been declared for Friday (5/8 & 5/9).  There is a test we would like to run tomorrow and Friday which falls under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing of commercial products:  Section 321, subsections 7 & 8.  
 
The planned testing is fire propagation testing resulting from simulated electrical shorts to ground using ‘moss’ media.  We used an emissions factor from AP-42 for open burning of forest residue; hemlock, cedar, douglas fir.  The simulated moss is commercially available floral arrangement craft material made from long strand shavings of aspen tree. 
 
Fuel Mass: 0.661 lb  (300 g)
Emissions factor, particulate: 4 lb/ton 
Generated emissions, particulate: 0.001323 lb</td><td>05/07/2025</td><td>05/08/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Facility Contact/Quality and EH&S Manager - James Busch submitted via email a request to conduct testing: fire propagation testing resulting from simulated electrical shorts to ground using 'moss' media. 
The testing will be conducted on a High Pollution Advisory (HPA) Day/restricted burn period (May 8 - 9, 2025). The email request was received on May 7, 2025, at 4:18 p.m.

A HPA Day was declared on May 8, 2025. The restricted burn period runs from May 1 - September 30. 

Testing conducted on a HPA Day/restricted burn period must emit less than 2 pounds of particulate matter.

Mr. Busch informed in this email that the testing would generate less than 2 pounds of particulate matter, values provided anticipated 0.001323 pounds of particulate emissions will be burned from the testing.

As this value provided falls within the allowable limit, the testing was approved on May 8, 2025, at 7:35 a.m.

All requirements in Maricopa County Air Pollution Regulations Rule 314 § 321 have been satisfied and will result in a compliant report.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT010219</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>We have been requested to continue this previously approved testing (below) and are seeking an additional authorization through the 16th of May (e.g., 5/7-5/16).

The Arizona Department of Environmental Quality website indicates a high pollution advisory with a restricted burn period has been declared for tomorrow and the next day (5/8 & 5/9).  Should weather patterns hold, it will potentially extend through parts of the next week.  There is a series of tests in discussion which would occur periodically over the next week, including today and tomorrow, which falls under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing of commercial products:  Section 321, subsections 7 & 8.  
 
The planned schedule for each applicable day is stress testing up to three small format lithium-ion batteries weighing 5.6 pounds each which, if they all catch fire during the testing, would emit less than the 2 lbs allowed during a restricted burn period under those rules.  We used a calculated emissions factor from peer reviewed publications (attached).  This emission factor is more conservative and less permissive than AP42: Open Burning - Vehicle Parts by a factor of two (dependent upon testing).  
 
Batteries initial mass per day (potential):  16.8 lbs 
High end of historical mass loss (e.g. fuel): 40%
Emission factor: 11.1%
(initial mass)*(mass loss %)*(emission factor) = 0.746 pounds of particulate matter</td><td>05/07/2025</td><td>05/08/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Facility Contact/Quality and EH&S Manager - James Busch submitted via email a request to conduct testing: stress testing of small format lithium-ion batteries. 

The testing will be conducted on a High Pollution Advisory (HPA) Day/restricted burn period.

The email request was received on May 7, 2025, at 4:16 p.m.

A HPA Day was declared on May 8, 2025. The restricted burn period runs from May 1 - September 30.

Testing conducted on a HPA Day/restricted burn period must emit less than 2 pounds of particulate matter.

Mr. Busch informed in this email that the testing would generate less than 2 pounds of particulate matter, values provided anticipated 0.746 pounds of particulate emissions will be burned from the testing.

As this value provided falls within the allowable limit, the testing was approved on May 8, 2025, at 7:37 a.m.

All requirements in Maricopa County Air Pollution Regulations Rule 314 § 321 have been satisfied and will result in a compliant report.</td></tr>
