<tr><td>CRPT010108</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Note: activities approved via email that is attached.

The Arizona Department of Environmental Quality website indicates a high pollution advisory with a restricted burn period has been declared for today and tomorrow (4/10-4/11).  Should weather patterns hold, it will potentially extend through parts of the next two weeks.  There is a series of tests in discussion which would occur periodically over the next two weeks, including today and tomorrow, which falls under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing of commercial products:  Section 321, subsections 7 & 8.  
 
The planned schedule for each applicable day is stress testing up to three small format lithium-ion batteries weighing 5.6 pounds each which, if they all catch fire during the testing, would emit less than the 2 lbs allowed during a restricted burn period under those rules.  We used a calculated emissions factor from peer reviewed publications (attached).  This emission factor is more conservative and less permissive than AP42: Open Burning - Vehicle Parts by a factor of two (dependent upon testing).  
 
Batteries initial mass per day (potential):  16.8 lbs 
High end of historical mass loss (e.g. fuel): 40%
Emission factor: 11.1%
(initial mass)*(mass loss %)*(emission factor) = 0.746 pounds of particulate matter</td><td>04/18/2025</td><td>04/22/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Facility Contact/Quality and EH&S Manager - James Busch submitted via email a request to conduct a series of testing: Stress testing of small lithium-ion batteries is being tested.

The stress testing will be conducted on a High Pollution Advisory (HPA) Day. The email request was received on April 10, 2025, at 10:11 a.m.

A HPA Day was declared for the proposed dates of testing on April 10, 2025.

Testing conducted on a HPA Day must emit less than 2 pounds of particulate matter.

Mr. Busch informed in this email that the testing would generate less than 2 pounds of particulate matter, values provided anticipated 0.746 pounds of particulate emissions will be burned from the stress testing. 

As this value provided falls within the allowable limit, the testing was approved on April 10, 2025, at 10:32 a.m.

All requirements in Maricopa County Air Pollution Regulations Rule 314 § 321 have been satisfied and will result in a compliant report.</td></tr>
