<tr><td>CRPT008856</td><td>F006261</td><td>Microsoft Corp - Broadway Rd</td><td>Synthetic Minor</td><td>Data Centers (&gt;100 MW)</td><td>CMP003908</td><td>Microsoft Corporation</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Startup Notification</td><td>12/27/24 Startup notification for Emergency Generators at PHX12 COLO 1 at Broadway Road</td><td>01/08/2025</td><td>02/27/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT009452</td><td>F006261</td><td>Microsoft Corp - Broadway Rd</td><td>Synthetic Minor</td><td>Data Centers (&gt;100 MW)</td><td>CMP003908</td><td>Microsoft Corporation</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Test Report</td><td>Microsoft is submitting the attached source test report for a representative Cummins QSK95-G9 engine equipped with an SCR device installed at the permitted Broadway Road datacenter in Goodyear, AZ (Facility identification no. F006261, Existing Permit no. P0009934/ P0011358). The source test was completed on September 22, 2023, at the Cummins engine packaging facility in Batavia, OH. A site-specific test plan was submitted to MCAQD on August 11, 2023, outlining the procedures that were used to conduct the stack test. Testing was conducted to verify compliance with the permitted NOx BACT standard of 0.5 g/bhp-hr. Average results for the stack test were 0.40 g/bhp-hr. Note this source test was originally submitted to Maricopa County in relation to the Olive Avenue location on 11/3/2023.  Microsoft received the Maricopa County review letter for the submitted test report on August 14, 2024.</td><td>01/08/2025</td><td>04/10/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT009467</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>The Arizona Department of Environmental Quality website indicates a high pollution advisory with a restricted burn period has been declared for today.  Should weather patterns hold, it will potentially extend through tomorrow (Jan. 8th & 9th).  There are two tests in discussion for these days which fall under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing related to commercial product testing:  Sections 321, subsections 7 & 8.  
 
The first test, scheduled for today, will be stress testing a single medium format lithium-ion batteries weighing 28.5 pounds which, if it catches fire during the testing, would emit less than the 2 lbs allowed during a restricted burn period under those rules.  We used a calculated emissions factor from peer reviewed publications (attached).  This emission factor is more conservative and less permissive than AP42: Open Burning - Vehicle Parts by a factor of two (dependent upon testing).  
 
Batteries initial mass:  26.5 lbs 
High end of historical mass loss (e.g. fuel): 40%
Emission factor: 11.1%
(initial mass)*(mass loss %)*(emission factor) = 1.18 pounds of particulate matter 

The second series of tests, scheduled for today and tomorrow, is flammability testing to characterize gaseous VOC blends for application under ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 34-2001.  Each test will burn a stoichiometric mixture of VOCs: test type A burns 0.545 lbs and test type B burns 0.302 lbs.  Up to four tests will be conducted daily for a total fuel mass of less than 2 lbs per day.   Burning of the gaseous VOC samples in stoichiometric mixtures is not expected to emit any particulate.</td><td>01/08/2025</td><td>01/08/2025</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Facility Contact/Quality and EH&S Manager - James Busch submitted via email a request to conduct two series of testing: Stress testing of small lithium-ion batteries and flammability testing to characterize gaseous VOC blends for application under ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 34-2001. 

The stress testing and/or flammability testing to characterize gaseous VOC blends for application under ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 34-2001 will be conducted on a High Pollution Advisory (HPA) Day. The email request was received on January 8, 2025, at 10:31 a.m. 

A HPA Day was declared for the proposed dates of testing (January 8, 2025) for PM10.

Testing conducted on a HPA Day must emit less than 2 pounds of particulate matter.

Mr. Busch informed in this email that the testing would generate less than 2 pounds of particulate matter, values provided anticipated 1.18 pounds of particulate emissions will be burned from the stress testing. Burning of the gaseous VOC samples in stoichiometric mixtures is not expected to emit any particulate matter. 

As this value provided falls within the allowable limit, the testing was approved on January 8, 2025, at 12:54 p.m.

All requirements in Maricopa County Air Pollution Regulations Rule 314 § 321 have been satisfied and will result in a compliant report.</td></tr>
