<tr><td>CRPT009280</td><td>F000015</td><td>Salt River Project - Kyrene Generating Station</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP015964</td><td>Salt River Project</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>O&M Plan</td><td>Enclosed please find the revised Operation and Maintenance (O&M) Plan for the continuous emissions monitoring system (CEMS) at Salt River Project’s (SRP) Kyrene Generating Station (Kyrene). The revisions to the O&M Plan include:
•	Format changes and minor verbiage changes.
•	Removal of the permit number reference.
•	Revision of the CO and NOx Part 60 QA CGA performance specification criteria to 2 ppm for analyzer spans less than or equal to 20 ppm.
•	Removal of the Total Inlet Sample (FM-7) and NOx Duct (FM-8) parameters from the CEMS daily and weekly checklists. Omission of these parameters does not result in operational deficiencies.
•	Revision of the Daily Checklist flow rate operating ranges to ensure consistency with Section II of the CEMS O&M Plan and on-site operation. 
•	Removal of the NOx Stack (FM-3), CO Stack (FM-4), and O2 Stack (FM-2) parameters from the weekly checklist, as these are monitored and recorded daily.</td><td>11/26/2024</td><td>12/10/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Approved Revised CEMS O&M and QA plan</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT009281</td><td>F049993</td><td>Talis Construction Portable Crushing Yard</td><td>Minor</td><td>Crushing and Screening, Portable</td><td>CMP004625</td><td>Talis Construction Corporation</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Dust Control Plan</td><td>Rule 316 Dust Control Plan for Center St Crushing Yard at 2412 N. Center St, Mesa, AZ 85201</td><td>11/26/2024</td><td>11/27/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>R316 DCP submitted and associated with move notice</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT009278</td><td>F002622</td><td>Nammo Talley</td><td>Minor</td><td>Chemical Manufacture, Processing, etc</td><td>CMP002276</td><td>Nammo Defense Systems Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Soil Vapor Extraction Unit Test Report</td><td>10.24.24 SVE Performance Test Report (note-sampling method will be EPA 8015 for next reporting)</td><td>11/26/2024</td><td>12/10/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>61.g: The carbon adsorption system achieved at least 90% removal efficiency for 1,1 - DCE and TCE. All other compounds were found to be below detectable thresholds.

63.a.i: Grab samples are taken at the influent and effluent of the soil vapor extraction unit (SVEU).
63.a.ii: Samples are analyzed using EPA method 8260B, which is not equivalent to EPA method 8015, nor was this testing method approved by the control officer as required by the facility's permit. However, the facility provided its raw data from the sampling, which demonstrated 90% or greater removal efficiency for all detected VOCs. Going forward, the facility has agreed to use EPA method 8015, as required in the facility's permit.
63.a.iii: The volumetric flow rate of gas streams into the soil vapor extraction unit was measured and found to be 122 square cubic feet per minute (scfm).

63.c.i: The date of sampling, type of air pollution control in use and the name of the company preforming the laboratory analysis was recorded.
63.c.ii: The concentration of the VOC at the inlet of the SVEU was recorded.
63.c.iii: The emission rate of the VOC exiting the SVEU was recorded.
63.c.iv: The VOC efficiency for the SVEU was recorded.

63.f: The performance test was conducted on October 24, 2024, and submitted to MCAQD for review on November 26, 2024; therefore, the results of the test were submitted within 45-days of the test being performed as specified in the facility's permit.</td></tr>
