<tr><td>CRPT009053</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Notification</td><td>The Arizona Department of Environmental Quality’s Air Quality Forecast webpage indicates that a high pollution advisory and restricted burn period has been issued for today and tomorrow, October 10th and 11th.  A series of tests, detailed below, are scheduled for today and tomorrow which fall under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Maricopa County Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing related to DOT/DOD/commercial/other requirements (Sections 320/321, subsections 7 & 8). 

We will be stress testing a series of small lithium-ion batteries which, if they catch fire during the testing, would emit less than the 2 lbs (aggregate) allowed during a restricted burn period under those rules.  We use a calculated emissions factor from a peer reviewed publication which is more conservative and less permissive than ‘AP42: Vehicle Parts’ by a factor of two.  

Battery Weight	12.32	lbs
Fraction of Consumable Mass Lost	0.4	
Particulate Emission Rate (lb/lb)	0.111	
Particulate Emissions per test	0.547	lbs
		
repetition	3	
Aggregate Particulate Emissions	1.641	lbs</td><td>10/10/2024</td><td>10/10/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Facility Contact/Senior Environmental Health & Safety Specialist - James Busch submitted via email a request to conduct stress testing of small lithium-ion batteries on a High Pollution Advisory Day. The email request was received on October 10, 2024, at 2:03 p.m., a High Pollution Advisory (HPA) Day was declared for the proposed dates of testing (October 10 - 11, 2024) for Ozone.

Testing conducted on a HPA Day must emit less than 2 pounds of particulate matter.

Mr. Busch informed in this email that the testing would generate less than 2 pounds of particulate matter, values provided anticipated 1.641 pounds of aggregate particulate emissions will be burned per day of testing. 

As this value provided falls within the allowable limit, the testing was approved on October 10, 2024 at 2:21 p.m.

All requirements in Maricopa County Air Pollution Regulations Rule 314 § 320 have been satisfied and will result in a compliant report.</td></tr>
