<tr><td>CRPT008861</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Notification</td><td>The Arizona Department of Environmental Quality’s Air Quality Forecast webpage indicates that a high pollution advisory and restricted burn period has been issued for today and tomorrow (8/27 & 8/28, 2024).  Additionally, Thursday 8/29 has a high pollution warning which may become a high pollution advisory with a restricted burn period.   A series of tests, detailed below, is scheduled for those days falls under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Maricopa County Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing related to DOT/DOD/commercial/other requirements (Sections 320/321, subsections 7 & 8). 

Exponent intends to conduct flammability testing to characterize gaseous VOC blends for application under ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 34-2001.  Each test will burn a stoichiometric mixture of VOCs: test type A burns 0.545 pounds and test type B burns 0.337 pounds.  Up to two tests will be conducted daily for a total of daily fuel mass of less than 1.09 pounds.  

Burning of the gaseous VOC samples in stoichiometric mixtures is not expected to emit any particulate. Additionally, the total fuel mass (1.09 pounds) for the series of tests is less that the 2lb, which could not conceivably generate more than 2lbs of particulate during testing.</td><td>08/30/2024</td><td>09/03/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Facility Contact/Senior Environmental Health & Safety Specialist - James Busch submitted via email a request to conduct flammability testing to characterize gaseous VOC samples on a High Pollution Advisory Day. The email request was received on August 27, 2024, at 12:48 p.m., a High Pollution Advisory (HPA) Day was declared for the proposed dates of testing (August 27 - 29, 2024) for Ozone. 

Testing conducted on a HPA Day must emit less than 2 pounds of particulate matter.

Mr. Busch informed in this email that the testing would generate less than 2 pounds of particulate matter, values provided anticipated 1.09 pounds of fuel mass will be burned per day of testing. As this value provided falls within the allowable limit, the testing was approved on August 27, 2024 at 1:39 p.m.

All requirements in Maricopa County Air Pollution Regulations Rule 314 § 320 have been satisfied and will result in a compliant report.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT008859</td><td>F000037</td><td>Palo Verde Nuclear Generating Station</td><td>Synthetic Minor</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP003677</td><td>Arizona Public Service Company</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Update to the Unit 3A EDG Hour Meter failure reported under CRPT008784. Hour meter was repaired and determined operational as of August 28, 2024.</td><td>08/30/2024</td><td>09/05/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Review of the facility notification regarding the repair of the non-resetting totalizing hour meter for a facility emergency standby internal combustion engine, unit 3A (ENG005). The notification confirmed that the hour meter unit was restored to service on August 28, 2024. The facility maintained engine runtime records during the non-resetting hour meter unit downtime, August 1, 2024 - August 28, 2024.

No compliance issues noted.


Refer to INSP201228 for compliance determination.</td></tr>
