<tr><td>CRPT008612</td><td>F040194</td><td>PH Data Center</td><td>Minor</td><td>Data Centers (&gt;100 MW)</td><td>CMP020492</td><td>NTT Global Data Centers Americas Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Periodic Reporting</td><td>The purpose of this letter is to submit the required semi-annual nitrogen oxides (NOx) report (Report) for the NTT Global Data Centers Americas, Inc. (NTT) PH1 and PH2 data centers [Facility (Facility Number: F040194)] to Maricopa County Air Quality Department (MCAQD). This Report covers the reporting period January 1, 2024, through June 30, 2024. The Facility currently operates under a Non-Title V Air Quality Permit to Operate and/or Construct Permit [Permit Number: P0009797 (Permit)], which was issued on August 26, 2021, and subsequently revised on October 12, 2023.</td><td>08/13/2024</td><td>08/23/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>The subject Semiannual NOx emissions report is required by permit condition 9 for which my compliance determination follows:

Subject report submitted 44 days following end of the reporting period (June 30, 2024).

Report verified to include the monthly and 12-month rolling total emissions of NOx for the preceding 12 months i.e. July 2023 through June 2024.
     Table 7: Facility-wide Monthly and 12-month rolling NOx emissions
                   (highest month) January 2024: 6.45 tons

Report verified to include monthly and 12 month rolling usage of the total hours of operation and fuel usage for each emergency engine for the preceding 12 months as follows:

Facility limited reporting to the engines installed rather than the engines permitted as follows:
PH1: (26 ICE)
    GEN11 A,B,C,D
    GEN12 A,B,C,D
    GEN13 A,B,C,D
    GEN14 A,B,C,D
    GEN15 A,B,C,D
    GEN16 A,B,C,D
    GEN1-H
    GEN-Z
PH2: (37 ICE)
    GEN21 A,B,C,D
    GEN22 A,B,C,D
    GEN23 A,B,C,D (GEN23 A startup notice is not on file - However, hours of operation is reported as zero.)
    GEN24 A,B,C,D
    GEN25 A,B,C,D
    GEN26 A,B,C,D
    GEN2-H

NOTE 1: The report stated the NOx emissions were calculated with a revised permit condition 1.c. Equation 4 .  I contacted the permit engineer supervisor, Ryan Dalrymple and he asked me to let you know he is reopening the permit to correct equation 4; and noted for me the report's citing of model Kohler KD3250 in PH1 is because of they are citing the model of the generator rather than the engine.

NOTE 2: The previously submitted startup notifications delineate both "Permit Company Equipment ID" and "Facility Equipment ID" which are disparate and unnecessarily confusing because the numbers are similar i.e. GEN17-A  :: GEN24-A, et al.
Also, as in NOTE 1 above, another purpose of the key is to establish consistent naming convention for each engine. 

I solemnly suggest the facility draft a key and report using only one of the equipment ID's, The key may be included as an attachment to any report it is needed for.

I telephoned Kurt Schriever and informed him of my observations and the above NOTES. He explained each datacenter has approximately 25 ICE and the facility has potential to expand to over 170 ICE in total.

No violations were noted, but compliance verification limited to permit condition 9.</td></tr>
