Enclosed please find the Semiannual Excess Emissions and Monitor Downtime Report for Arizona State’s original Combined Cycle Stationary Gas Turbine as required by permit conditions 31.a and 31.b of ASU Tempe’s air quality permit. The reporting period for the report is January 1, 2024, through June 30, 2024.

Please contact either Michael Ochs, Director Safety in the Built Environment, EHS at 480-965-3580 or Mary Cuevas-Delgado, Safety Partner, EHS at 480-228-5251 if you have any questions.

Sincerely,
Morgan R. Olsen
Executive Vice President, Treasurer and CFO</td><td>07/30/2024</td><td>08/16/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>This Semiannual Excess Emissions and Monitor Downtime report is for January through June 2024. This was submitted as required by permit condition 30.a. The report was submitted within 30 days following the end of the reporting period per permit condition 30.c. The report includes a statement that no excess emissions have occurred and identifies dates and times that the continuous monitoring system was down and the reason why as required by permit condition 30.b. 

No compliance issues were noted.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT008684</td><td>F002999</td><td>Sanctuary On Camelback Mountain</td><td>Minor</td><td>External Fuel Burning Equipment (ATO)</td><td>CMP026755</td><td>5700 MCD Assets II LLC</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Ownership Transfer</td><td>Transfer Application</td><td>07/30/2024</td><td>08/06/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Gladys Kaider (Director of Finance) with Sanctuary Camelback Mountain submitted Permit Transfer Application via AQPermits</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT008682</td><td>F001522</td><td>Mesquite Generating Station</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP002166</td><td>Mesquite Power LLC</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Title V Compliance Certification</td><td>Enclosed for your review, please find the Compliance Certification and Semiannual Monitoring Report from Mesquite Generating Station covering the reporting time period from January 1, 2024 to June 30, 2024, as required in the applicable Title V Permit condition 21.c. If you have any questions regarding this report, please contact D’Armond Chee at (602) 236-0039.</td><td>07/30/2024</td><td>10/07/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>The report was submitted within the appropriate time frame.

The report should include, at a minimum, the following:

1) The identification of each term or condition of the permit that is the basis of the certification;
2) The compliance status;
3) Whether compliance was continuous or intermittent;
4) The method(s) used for determining the compliance status of the source, currently and over the reporting period;
5) Other facts as the Control Officer may require to determine compliance status of the source;
6) Each report shall cover all instances of deviations from these permit conditions during the reporting period, the cause of the deviations if any were present, and any applicable corrective actions taken;
7) The Permittee shall include a copy of the portion of the odor log which covers the applicable 6 month reporting period in each of the semiannual compliance reports. If no complaints were received during the reporting period, a statement to that effect may be substituted for the copy of the odor log; and
8) Permittee shall comply with applicable reporting requirements for support operations including spray coating Architectural Coatings (Permit Condition 24), Abrasive Blasting (Permit Condition 25), Spray Coating (Permit
Condition 26), Solvent Cleaning Operations (Permit Condition 27), and Volatile Organic Compounds (Permit Condition 28).
9) Visible emissions and opacity readings

The format of the report satisfies items (1), (2), (3), and (4) from the list above. Each permit condition was listed with statements demonstrating the facility was In Compliance and denoting that the compliance status was
continuous. An intermittent status was noted for the Combined Cycle Emissions Limits during the timeframe from February 29, 2024 - June 30, 2024. Unit 2 exceeded the 22.2 lb/hr NOx 1-hr average on 6/26/2024 at 0800 and the 2.5 ppn NOx 3-hr average between 0700 and 1000 on 6/26/2024. The 24-hr and 72-hr reports for the 6/26/2024 excess emissions event were submitted as required.

The method used for determining the compliance status was also detailed for each permit condition.

Item (6) - The report demonstrates the facility was in compliance with each permit condition. The facility denotes one deviation from permit condition 18.c.i., Tables 18.2 and 18.3. Deviation occurred on 6/26/2024. 

Item (7) - The report states there were no odor log entries during the reporting period.

Item (8) - The report indicates the facility is complying with the reporting requirements for operations listed in Permit Conditions 24-28. The report states the status of each of the operations on site for the reporting period.

Item (9) - The report states the facility logged visible emissions testing and that there were no visible emissions observed other than uncombined water.

No compliance issues were noted.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT008681</td><td>F001522</td><td>Mesquite Generating Station</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP002166</td><td>Mesquite Power LLC</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Periodic Reporting</td><td>Enclosed for your review, please find the attached Semiannual Excess Emissions and Monitoring System Performance Report from Mesquite Generating Station reflecting the period from January 1, 2024 to June 30, 2024. This report is submitted in accordance with 40CFR60.7(c) and (d). Data in the report reflects all plant operations for the entire reporting period for the Units 1, 2, 5, and 6. If you have any questions regarding this report, please call D’Armond Chee at 602-236-0039.</td><td>07/30/2024</td><td>10/04/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>The report was submitted within the appropriate time frame.

The facility is required to submit only the summary report form if the total duration of excess emissions for the reporting period was reported to be less than 1 percent of the total operating time and the total duration of CEMS downtime for the reporting period was reported to be less than 5 percent of the total operating time for all CCS units.

The facility reported there were no NOx and CO emissions excursions or exceedances for CCS Unit 1, 5, and 6 during the reporting period of January 1, 2024 to June 30, 2024.Therefore, the total duration of excess emissions for the reporting period was reported to be less than 1 percent of the total operating time for CCS Units 1, 5 and 6.  

The facility reported a NOx emission exceedance for CCS Unit 2 during the reporting period of January 1, 2024 to June 30, 2024. 1 hour of NOx emission exceedance and 6 hours of NOx emissions corrected to 15 percent O2 was reported. The total duration of excess NOx emissions for the reporting period was reported to be 7 hours. The total percent of operating time was calculated to be 0.61 percent. The total duration of excess NOx emissions for the reporting period was less than 1 percent of the total operating time.

The total duration of CEMS downtime for the reporting period was reported to be less than 5 percent of the total operating time for CCS Units 1, 2, 5 and 6. The highest reported duration of CEMS downtime across all units was for CCS Unit #5 (pollutant was NOx), at 0.99% of total operating time. The total duration of CEMS downtown for all units for both NOx and CO was calculated to be 4.08%.

The facility has complied with the reporting requirements of 40 CFR §60.7(c) and (d) by only submitting the summary report form for the reporting period.

Reference inspection report INSP202662 for more detail.</td></tr>
