<tr><td>CRPT008559</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Notification</td><td>Bryan, Brandon, Frank, and Scott: 
 
The county air quality department website indicates a high pollution advisory with a restricted burn period has been declared this afternoon. There is testing scheduled for this afternoon which falls under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing related to DOT/DOD/commercial/other requirements:  Sections 320/321, subsections 7 & 8.  
 
We will be stress testing a small lithium-ion battery which, if it catches fire during the testing, would emit less than the 2 lbs allowed during a restricted burn period under those rules even when considering the previously approved testing (see attached).  We calculate an emissions factor from a peer reviewed publication (attached).  This is more conservative and less permissive than AP42: Vehicle Parts by a factor of two (dependent upon testing).  
 
Battery’s initial mass:  500 grams
High end of historical mass loss (e.g. fuel): 40%
Emission factor: 11.1%
(initial mass)*(mass loss %)*(emission factor) = 22 grams or about 0.05 pounds of particulate matter (this test may be conducted twice, and if so, total would be 0.1 pounds of particulate matter)

I am in the process of creating a compliance report in CROMERR / IMPACT which you should see soon.
 
Thank you, 
Kevin Reichelderfer</td><td>07/12/2024</td><td>07/12/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Facility F000397 - Exponent 

Facility Contact/Senior Environmental Health & Safety Specialist - James Busch submitted via email a request for testing of small lithium-ion batteries on a High Pollution Advisory Day. The email request was received on July 12, 202 at 1:45 p.m., a High Pollution Advisory (HPA) Day was declared for the proposed date of testing (July 12, 2024) for Ozone. Testing conducted on a HPA Day must emit less than 2 pounds of particulate matter. 

Mr. Busch informed in this email that the testing would generate less than 2 pounds of particulate matter, values provided anticipated 0.1 pounds of particulate matter. As this value provided falls within the allowable limit, the testing was approved on July 12, 2024 at 3:11 p.m.

All requirements in Maricopa County Air Pollution Regulations Rule 314 § 320 have been satisfied and will result in a compliant report.</td></tr>
