<tr><td>CRPT008548</td><td>F000415</td><td>Progressive Roofing</td><td>Minor</td><td>Tar Kettle/ Asphalt Day Tanker, Portable</td><td>CMP002602</td><td>Progressive Services Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Portable Equipment Move Notice</td><td>Move kettle</td><td>07/10/2024</td><td>07/10/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Wendy Barcala filed two Portable Equipment Move Notices that their portable equipment will be moved from their office base at 23 N 35th Ave to a vacant commercial parcel at 13028 W Rancho Santa Fe Blvd in Avondale.  Equipment moved on Sunday, June 30th and be located there till September 30th.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT008547</td><td>F002372</td><td>Gro-Well Brands</td><td>Minor</td><td>Other (Miscellaneous)</td><td>CMP003575</td><td>Western Organics Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Periodic Reporting</td><td>Q2 2024 Fire Report</td><td>07/10/2024</td><td>07/23/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>I telephoned the site contact, Jade Navarro about the report.

Mr. Navarro explained the following:
• Grinding is an important temperature control measure.
• The Overs Pile is the most common pile to ignite. The reason for this is because it is often
   contaminated with rocks which bring the temperature of the pile up because it inhibits
   readily grinding the pile.
• The height of the Overs Pile is dynamic on a daily basis but is generally 10-13 feet in height.

Mr. Navarro stated the facility has commenced operating two grinders in order to increase the volume of material grinding and in the long run is monitoring the progress of third-party off-site testing of rock removal equipment; for which if successful may be an effective tool toward more effective temperature controls.

I compared the compliance report with most recent open burning complaint i.e. CMPL027704 for which facility reported the June 16, 2024 fire was in the Compost C pile. Mr. Navarro clarified the pile ignited in the Overs pile and due to wind spread to Compost Pile C.

• I suggested including a copy of the daily temperature log for each respective fire occurrence with the Quarterly
   Fire Report.
• I suggested supplementing the daily temperature log with the estimated pile height/row length.

No violations noted.</td></tr>
