<tr><td>CRPT008371</td><td>F001582</td><td>Vulcan Materials Co-Western Division</td><td>Minor</td><td>Nonmetallic Mineral Processing</td><td>CMP003522</td><td>Vulcan Materials Company</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Test Report</td><td>Performance Test Report for Vulcan 43rd Ave conducted on 5/2.  The end of the report contains an email sent by MCAQD with a new permit.  This report reflects the new emission limits issued by the current permit.</td><td>06/05/2024</td><td>09/23/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT008379</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Notification</td><td>The county air quality department website indicates a high pollution advisory with a restricted burn period has been declared for tomorrow. There is testing scheduled for tomorrow which falls under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing related to DOT/DOD/commercial/other requirements:  Sections 320/321, subsections 7 & 8.  
 
We will be stress testing small lithium-ion batteries which, if they catch fire during the testing, would emit less than the 2 lbs allowed during a restricted burn period under those rules even when considering the previously approved testing (see attached).  We calculate an emissions factor from a peer reviewed publication (attached).  This is more conservative and less permissive than AP42: Vehicle Parts by a factor of two (dependent upon testing).  
 
Batteries initial mass:  total aggregate mass 5.7 lbs per day
High end of historical mass loss (e.g. fuel): 40%
Emission factor: 11.1%
(initial mass)*(mass loss %)*(emission factor) = 0.253 pounds of particulate matter</td><td>06/04/2024</td><td>06/12/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Facility F000397 - Exponent 

Facility Contact/Senior Environmental Health & Safety Specialist - James Busch submitted via email a request for testing of small lithium-ion batteries on a High Pollution Advisory Day. The email request was received on June 04, 2024 at 3:39 p.m., a High Pollution Advisory (HPA) Day was declared for the proposed date of testing (June 05, 2024) for Ozone. Testing conducted on a HPA Day must emit less than 2 pounds of particulate matter. 

Mr. Busch informed in this email that the testing would generate less than 2 pounds of particulate matter, values provided anticipated 0.253 pounds of particulate matter. As this value provided falls within the allowable limit, the testing was approved on June 04, 2024 at 4:22 p.m.

All requirements in Maricopa County Air Pollution Regulations Rule 314 § 320 have been satisfied and will result in a compliant report.</td></tr>
