<tr><td>CRPT008136</td><td>F002126</td><td>Arlington Valley</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP000343</td><td>Arlington Valley LLC</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Title V Compliance Certification</td><td>Attached is the 1st Semi-Annual Title V certification and CEMS Excess Emissions and Monitoring Downtime Report. Each report has its own respective certification page.</td><td>04/18/2024</td><td>05/30/2024</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>This report was submitted for the period of October 1, 2023, through March 31, 2024. It was due April 30, 2024, and submitted on April 18, 2024.

They stated: “1) Calendar Date – During this compliance period there was one day on January 21st which natural gas was combusted in the duct burners for an entire 24-hour period. Included in Attachment 2 are daily emission tables from the data acquisition handling system (DAHS) listing hourly NOX emission rates (lb/MW-hr) demonstrating partial operations each calendar day during the compliance period. 
2) Average Nitrogen Oxides (NOX) Emission Rates - Hourly average NOX emissions rates (Ib/MW-hr) are provided in Attachment 2 and Attachment 3. There were no periods of non-compliance with the emission limits. 
3) Monitor Availability — During this compliance period there was one day on January 21st which natural gas was combusted in the duct burners for an entire 24-hour period. Even so, both the NOX and diluent (oxygen, 02) continuous emission monitoring systems (CEMS) obtained valid data for at least 75 percent of the unit operating hours in at least 22 of every 30 successive combustion turbine system (CTG-1 and CTG-2) operating days during this compliance period.”

Sufficient data appeared to have been recorded throughout the entire period, satisfying compliance with 21.b.i of P0007591. 

21.d.i – Facility claims they are in compliance with all conditions.
21.d.ii – No deviations were recorded in the Report Certification.
21.d.iii – Observed to be in compliance
21.d.iv – The facility uses EPA Method 9 to test for visible emissions. No visible emissions were observed throughout this period.
21.d.v – No odor complaints were received.
21.d.vi – Attachment 5 confirmed they are utilizing ultra low sulfur diesel. 
21.d.vii – Turbo mister hours at 0.
21.d.viii and ix – CEMS Excess emissions and monitor downtime reports are in Attachment 4

Attachment 2 appears to be compliant for hourly emissions for Units 1 and 2 for NOx. 
Attachment 3 appears to be compliant for hourly emissions during startup, shutdown, tuning and testing.
Attachment 4 confirms there are 0% excess emissions that have been recorded during this time frame. Both CEMs units had downtime in reporting due to Calibration/Quality Assurance or Unknown causes, only unknown for GT-1. Any downtime that any of the CEMS experienced, stayed below 1% of the total operation time. If it went over 1%, this would be of concern for the CEMS operations.

No concerns noted.</td></tr>
