<tr><td>CRPT006836</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Notification</td><td>The county air quality department website indicates a high pollution advisory with a restricted burn period has been declared for today. There is new and additional testing scheduled for today which falls under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing related to DOT/DOD/commercial/other requirements:  Sections 320/321, subsections 7 & 8.  
 
We will be stress testing small lithium-ion batteries which, if they catch fire during the testing, would emit less than the 2 lbs allowed during a restricted burn period under those rules even when considering the previously approved testing (see attached).  We calculate an emissions factor from a peer reviewed publication (attached).  This is more conservative and less permissive than AP42: Vehicle Parts by a factor of two (dependent upon testing).  We will be initiating testing in the morning when air quality is better.
 
Battery initial mass:  total aggregate mass 19.8 lbs
High end of historical mass loss (e.g. fuel): 40%
