<tr><td>CRPT006831</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Notification</td><td>Bryan, Brandon, Frank (and Scott)  – 
 
The county air quality department website indicates that a high pollution advisory and restricted burn period has been issued for Wednesday 8/2/2023.  A test, detailed below, is scheduled for that day and falls under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing related to DOT/DOD/commercial/other requirements (Sections 320/321, subsections 7 & 8). 
 
We will be performing flame propagation testing by periodically dripping small amounts of 87 Octane gasoline, totaling up to 345 grams, on and near a heated engine exhaust manifold.  Any flame that initiates is immediately extinguished by water and/or fire extinguishers. 
 
To characterize the potential emissions and we have selected an emission factor related to boilers (uncontrolled) using distillate fuel oil (e.g. kerosene) from AP42 section 1.3 ‘Fuel Oil Combustion’ (attached).  This factor is a poor fit for the open combustion of gasoline but could be considered analogous to an extreme worst case scenario; it is selected due the absence of a more appropriate factor in AP42. 
 
The calculations are presented below.  The emissions for this small quantity of fuel, if it combusts, are near zero pounds.  
 
Fuel Oil Burning - Distillate 		
			
Initial Fuel Mass	345g (.761 lb)	 	 
Specific Gravity	0.76	 	 
Initial Volume	453 cm3 
(.050 gallons)	 	 
Sulfur content	30ppm (.00003 %)	 	 
			
Analyte	Emission Factor
lb/(1000 gallon * Sulfur%)	Emittance	 
SO2	142	5.12E-07	lbs
SO3	2	7.20E-09	lbs
NOx	20	7.20E-08	lbs
CO	5	1.80E-08	lbs
Filterable PM	2	7.20E-09	lbs
			
Analyte	Emission Factor
lb/(1000 gallon)	Emittance	 
Total Organics for Rating A	0.556	6.67E-05	lbs</td><td>08/01/2023</td><td>08/08/2023</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>On August 1, 2023 received an email correspondence from Senior Environmental Health & Safety Specialist, James Busch, requesting permission to conduct facility testing operations during the restricted burn period on August 2, 2023.  Submitted test report confirmed less than 2.0lbs of particulate matter would be emitted during test operations.  Facility request approved during restricted burn period.

No compliance issues noted.</td></tr>
