<tr><td>CRPT006688</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Notification</td><td>--Copied from the attached email--
The county air quality department website indicates that a high pollution watch has been issued for Monday 7/3/2023 which may become a high pollution advisory with a restricted burn period. A test, detailed below, is scheduled for Monday 7/3/2023 and falls under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing related to DOT/DOD/commercial/other requirements (Sections 320/321, subsections 7 & 8) even when combined with the emissions of previously approved testing (attached) which is also scheduled for that day. 

We will be performing flame propagation testing by dripping a small amount (145 grams) of 87 Octane gasoline on and near a heated engine exhaust manifold.  Any flame that initiates is immediately extinguished by water and/or fire extinguishers. 

To characterize the potential emissions and we have selected an emission factor related to boilers (uncontrolled) using distillate fuel oil (e.g. kerosene) from AP42 section 1.3 ‘Fuel Oil Combustion’ (attached).  This factor is a poor fit for the open combustion of gasoline but could be considered analogous to an extreme worst case scenario; it is selected due the absence of a more appropriate factor in AP42. 

The calculations are presented below.  The emissions for this small quantity of fuel, if it combusts, are near zero pounds and does not significantly contribute to the site’s emissions.  The previously approved testing could emit as much as 1.634 pounds of particulate (approval email attached). 

Fuel Oil Burning - Distillate 		
			
Initial Fuel Mass	145 g (.31967 lb)		
Specific Gravity	0.76		
Initial Volume	190 cm3 
(.050 gallons)		
Sulfur content	30ppm (.00003 %)		
			
Analyte	Emission Factor
lb/(1000 gallon * Sulfur%)	Emittance	 
SO2	142	2.15E-07	lbs
SO3	2	3.02E-09	lbs
NOx	20	3.02E-08	lbs
CO	5	7.56E-09	lbs
Filterable PM	2	3.02E-09	lbs
			
Analyte	Emission Factor
lb/(1000 gallon)	Emittance	 
Total Organics for Rating A	0.556	2.80E-05	lbs</td><td>06/30/2023</td><td>07/06/2023</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Email received on June 30, 2023 to notify department of additional facility testing during restricted burn period.  Submitted emission calculations from the facility confirmed compliance with County Rule 314.  

Facility testing operation approved from July 3 during restricted burn periods.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT006687</td><td>F001685</td><td>Southwest Asphalt</td><td>Synthetic Minor</td><td>Asphalt Plant, Portable</td><td>CMP001368</td><td>Fisher Sand & Gravel Co</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Non-Road Engine Determination</td><td>Attached is a copy of the non-road move notice for generator 040568 which is being removed from the site due to completion of its onsite needs.</td><td>06/30/2023</td><td>07/06/2023</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT006686</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Notification</td><td>The county air quality department website indicates today and much of the following days are restricted burn periods with a declared high pollution advisory or high pollution watches.  The testing scheduled over the next several day falls under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing related to DOT/DOD/commercial/other requirements:  Sections 320/321, subsections 7 & 8.  
 
We will be stress testing one medium size battery per day on the test schedule noted below which, if they catch fire during the testing, would emit less than 2 lbs allowed during a restricted burn period under those rules.  We calculate an emissions factor from a peer reviewed publication (attached).  This is more conservative and less permissive than AP42: Vehicle Parts by a factor of two.   

Emission Calculation – per battery 
Battery initial cell mass:  36.8 lbs each 
High end of historical mass loss (e.g. fuel): 40%
Emission factor: 11.1%
(initial mass)*(mass loss %)*(emission factor) = 1.634 pounds of particulate matter

Test Schedule - one battery per day on the following:
Saturday July 1st
Monday July 3rd 
Tuesday July 4th 
Wednesday July 5th
Thursday July 6th 
Friday July 7th</td><td>06/30/2023</td><td>07/06/2023</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Email received on June 30, 2023 to notify department of scheduled facility testing during restricted burn periods.  Submitted emission calculations from the facility confirmed compliance with County Rule 314.  

Facility testing operations approved from July 1-7 during restricted burn periods.</td></tr>
