<tr><td>CRPT006609</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Notification</td><td>The county air quality department website indicates tomorrow is a restricted burn period with a declared high pollution advisory.  The testing scheduled tomorrow falls under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing related to DOT/DOD/commercial/other requirements:  Sections 320/321, subsections 7 & 8.  
 
We will be stress testing two small lithium-ion battery which, if they catch fire during the testing, would emit less than the 2 lbs allowed during a restricted burn period under those rules.  We calculate an emissions factor from a peer reviewed publication (attached).  This is more conservative and less permissive than AP42: Vehicle Parts by a factor of two (dependent upon testing).   
 
Batteries initial mass:  1.09 lbs total
High end of historical mass loss (e.g. fuel): 40%
Emission factor: 11.1%
(initial mass)*(mass loss %)*(emission factor) = 0.048 pounds of particulate matter</td><td>06/08/2023</td><td>06/14/2023</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>After reviewing the information provided and the calculated emissions, The Department considered this event/notification to be compliant due to the low emissions calculated.</td></tr>
