<tr><td>CRPT006603</td><td>F043218</td><td>KB-1 Aggregate Mine Site</td><td>Minor</td><td>Crushing and Screening, Stationary</td><td>CMP003733</td><td>Custom Landscape Materials LLC</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Test Report</td><td>Method 9 Visible Emissions Test Result</td><td>06/08/2023</td><td>06/12/2023</td><td>No</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>The Maricopa County Air Quality Department is in receipt of the visible emissions test forms for KB-1 Aggregate Mine Site. Testing was conducted on 05/16/23 and the forms were submitted on 06/08/23. Unfortunately, the test cannot be accepted for the following reasons:

1.	Location – must include address
2.	Process equipment listed as “cone screen” – must obtain opacity readings from all required locations
3.	Control equipment – blank, would usually be spray bars/watering systems
4.	Describe emission point – blank, must be specific equipment and locations (i.e. outlet of cone crusher, conveyor to conveyor, outlet of shaker screen, etc)
5.	Describe emissions – use standard plume terminology
6.	Source layout sketch – not completed
7.	Data set – testing was not conducted properly, spaces are provided to take opacity readings from each location every 15 seconds, source to use one test form per location of readings

Method 9 readings consist of observations at 15 second intervals over a period of 6 minutes. Method 9 therefore requires 24 consecutive observations. Visible emissions testing shall be conducted at the following equipment: crushers, screening operations, and transfer points on belt conveyors.

Because the Method 9 test was not performed correctly, the facility is required to re-test. Please upload a new 7 day advance notification into the AQD Online Portal by the close of business Monday, 06/19/23. Visible emissions testing will be conducted and then the test forms shall be uploaded as a compliance report in the AQD Online Portal within 30 days of testing.

Source notified via email on 06/12/23.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT006602</td><td>F048033</td><td>Brenda's Inferno by The Armadillo Grill</td><td>Asbestos</td><td>Not Applicable</td><td>CMP004725</td><td>Embree Construction Group Inc</td><td>Asbestos</td><td>Asbestos inspection report</td><td>Test Date: 12/09/2023
NOtification Date 06/08/2023
Notable issues: NONE
Arizona Environmental Specialists (AES) is pleased to provide this report of findings to document the
laboratory results of the asbestos bulk samples collected from the above-mentioned address. AES
collected bulk samples from suspect asbestos-containing building materials that may be impacted during
upcoming renovation activities.
1. PROJECT INFORMATION
The subject property is identified a commercial property former restaurant “Brenda’s Inferno". Based on site
visit and correspondence with Mr. Hunter Prewit with Embree Group, activities may include the demolition
of the entire structure(s).
2. SCOPE OF SERVICES
The primary intent of AES’s services were to assist Embree Group in determining the presence, extent, and
condition of asbestos containing materials (ACM) associated with the subject structures scheduled for
demolition activities.
AES's services included a visual inspection of suspect materials, the collection, and analysis of samples to
determine the presence of ACM. The inspection included the identification of both friable and non-friable
building materials used in the construction of each of the structures scheduled for demolition. These
materials were classified as Regulated ACM (RACM), Category I non-friable ACM, or Category II nonfriable
ACM in accordance with National Emission Standard for Hazardous Air Pollutants (NESHAP)
regulations.
3. SURVEY PRODECURES
The field portion of our work included a visual inspection of accessible functional spaces and the collection
and analysis of samples of suspect ACM. On December 9th, 2022, AES performed asbestos inspection
work on the property. The field portion of the work was conducted by AES representative Mr. Bruce Karim,
certified EPA/AHERA Building Inspector.
3.1 Visual Inspection
The field portion of the inspection included the observation of accessible interior and exterior building
surfaces and finishes including concrete walkway and block wall. Observations were made to identify and
locate accessible suspect ACM. Suspect ACM are defined as those classes of materials which have, in the
past, been known to contain asbestos in some formulations.
During our walk-through, suspect materials were noted and grouped by homogeneous areas. A
"homogeneous area" (for bulk sampling purposes) is defined as an area that contains building materials,
suspect of containing asbestos that seem by texture, color, and wear to be uniform and applied or installed
during the same general time period. Our inspectors also evaluated the friability of each material by
physically touching each of the suspect materials. The general physical condition of suspect materials was
noted during the walk-through.
The materials listed below were identified during AES’s visual inspection and were considered suspect of
containing asbestos. The area designation preceding the material description indicates the Homogeneous
area number assigned to each suspect material. The materials highlighted in red bold text were determined
by laboratory analysis to contain asbestos in amounts greater than one percent.
3.2 Bulk Sampling
AES collected a total of 71 bulk samples from the suspect materials identified during the inspection. Samples
were collected in general accordance with AHERA sampling protocol EPA 40 CFR Part 763.86 Sampling.
Samples were labeled, the appropriate chain-of-custody documentation was completed, and the samples
were submitted to the laboratory.
Samples were collected from readily accessible construction materials which were representative of the
general construction of the subject areas and which were considered suspect ACM. Limited Destructive
activities were performed for the purpose of obtaining samples. However, no attempt was made to dismantle
equipment. Wall voids, building cavities, mechanical equipment parts, underground piping, may contain
unreported asbestos.</td><td>06/08/2023</td><td>06/16/2023</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Reviewed Asbestos NESHAP notification for 1902 E Camelback Rd. Phoenix, AZ.  See INSP180935. (F048033).</td></tr>
