<tr><td>CRPT006576</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Notification</td><td>The county air quality department website indicates tomorrow (6/2/2023) is a restricted burn period with a declared high pollution advisory.  The testing scheduled for tomorrow falls under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing related to DOT/DOD/commercial/other requirements:  Sections 320/321, subsections 7 & 8.  
 
We will be stress testing three small lithium-ion battery which, if they catch fire during the testing, would emit less than the 2 lbs allowed during a restricted burn period under those rules.  We calculate an emissions factor from a peer reviewed publication (attached).  This is more conservative and less permissive than AP42: Vehicle Parts by a factor of two (dependent upon testing).   
 
Battery initial mass:  three small batteries that combined weigh 2.38 lbs
High end of historical mass loss (e.g. fuel): 40%
Emission factor: 11.1%
(initial mass)*(mass loss %)*(emission factor) = 0.106 pounds of particulate matter</td><td>06/01/2023</td><td>06/05/2023</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>An email was received which shows The Department has approved this event. This report is an official approval.</td></tr>
