<tr><td>CRPT006525</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Test Notice</td><td>The county air quality department website indicates a high pollution advisory with a restricted burn period has been declared for tomorrow. There is testing scheduled tomorrow which falls under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing related to DOT/DOD/commercial/other requirements:  Sections 320/321, subsections 7 & 8.  
 
We will be stress testing small lithium-ion batteries which, if they catch fire during the testing, would emit less than the 2 lbs allowed during a restricted burn period under those rules.  We calculate an emissions factor from a peer reviewed publication (attached).  This is more conservative and less permissive than AP42: Vehicle Parts by a factor of two (dependent upon testing).  We will be initiating testing in the morning when air quality is better.
 
Battery initial mass:  total mass 13.02 lbs
High end of historical mass loss (e.g. fuel): 40%
Emission factor: 11.1%
(initial mass)*(mass loss %)*(emission factor) = 0.578 pounds of particulate matter</td><td>05/18/2023</td><td>06/05/2023</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>An email was received which shows the Maricopa County Air Quality Department has approved this test. This report is an official approval.</td></tr>
