<tr><td>CRPT006347</td><td>F002681</td><td>SFPP-Phoenix Terminal</td><td>Title V</td><td>Petroleum Products Bulk Storage/Handling</td><td>CMP002921</td><td>SFPP LP</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Loading Rack 8 Excess Emissions Report 4-9-23</td><td>04/11/2023</td><td>05/03/2023</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Excess emissions report due to loading rack incident on 09-Apr-23. Notification submitted to MCAQD staff in a timely manner on 10-Apr-23. Excess emissions report was submitted in a timely manner on 11-Apr-23. 

Per the report, excess emissions were created due to a transport truck driver failing to properly disconnect the loading arms and vapor hoses from loading rack #8 on 09-Apr-23 at approximately 1930 MST. The driver drove away while still connected, damaging the dry breaks on the loading arms and causing approximately 20gal of gasoline to spill onto engineered containment. SFPP estimates that the 20gal spill resulted in approximately 112lbs of VOC emissions, assuming 100% of the gasoline was evaporated. The report notes that the product was washed to an oil water separator and that the driver and truck are locked out of the facility until further investigation has been completed.

A Notice of Non-Compliance will be issued for the failure to prevent gasoline spillage by properly disconnecting the gasoline and vapor recovery hoses. See INSP180148 for further details.</td></tr>
