<tr><td>CRPT006200</td><td>F001759</td><td>Gila River Power Station</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP015964</td><td>Salt River Project</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>This letter serves to rescind the written notification submitted on March 6, 2023, that an excess emissions event related to Unit 1A at the Gila River Power Station occurred on October 26, 2022.</td><td>03/08/2023</td><td>03/10/2023</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>I reviewed Gila River Power Station notification letter dated March 8, 2023, to rescind excess emission notification submitted to the Air Quality Department on March 7, 2023 for 72-hour excess emissions in accordance with permit conditions 16.c.i.,16.f. and 18.a.iii. 

Gila River Power Station is rescinding the notification due to the invalid state of the Continuous Emissions Monitoring System (CEMS) data that did not provide warning or exceedance alarms to alert the Operations or Plant Environmentalist of the potential exceedance event. Upon the Gila River Power Station investigation of the invalidation, it was discovered that the minute data used to calculate the mass emission rate during startup was wrong. The gross calorific value (GCV) for the October 2022 sample of 1,062.7 Btu/scf was miscalculated to CO mass emission rate of 261.7 lb/hr and when the Data Acquisition and Handling Software (DAHS) was updated with the correct GCV, the CO mass emission was recalculated and the result was 256.8 lb/hr less than the allowable permit limit of 260 lb/hr.

See March 8, 2023 Subject: Rescinding of Excess Emissions Notification Facility ID: F001759-Gila River Power Station Permit No. P0009164 for more details.</td></tr>
