<tr><td>CRPT006094</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Notification</td><td>The county air quality department website indicates today is a restricted burn period with a declared high pollution advisory.  The testing scheduled this morning falls under the ‘shall approve’ portions of Rule 314 pertaining to safety testing related to DOT/DOD/commercial/other requirements:  Sections 320/321, subsections 7 & 8.  

We will be stress testing three small lithium-ion battery which, if they catch fire during the testing, would emit less than 2 lbs allowed during a restricted burn period under those rules.  We calculate an emissions factor from a peer reviewed publication (attached).  This is more conservative and less permissive than AP42: Vehicle Parts by a factor of two.  We will be initiating testing in the morning 10am and ending testing by 2pm when air quality is better. 

Battery initial mass:  2.5 lbs each (includes non-combustible material: total 7.5 lbs)
High end of historical mass loss (e.g. fuel):40%
Emission factor: 11.1%
(initial mass)*(mass loss %)*(emission factor) = 0.333 pounds of particulate matter emitted</td><td>02/14/2023</td><td>02/14/2023</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Notification submission to request testing operations during restricted burn period.

After reviewing facility correspondence and supporting documentation, the department has approved testing scheduled during the restricted burn period (PM10).

No noncompliance issues noted</td></tr>
