<tr><td>CRPT005866</td><td>F000014</td><td>Salt River Project - Agua Fria Generating Station</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP015964</td><td>Salt River Project</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>72-hour Excess Emissions Report for Agua Fria Unit 8. Initial notification made on January 10, 2023.</td><td>01/13/2023</td><td>01/18/2023</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>On January 10, 2023 at 11:56 a.m., the Permitee sent an email notifying the Department of an excess emissions event for Unit 8. The email satisfied Permit Condition 16.c.i. since the deviation was discovered January 9, 2023 during the 11:00 hour. This 72-hour excess emission report also satisfies P.C. 16.c.i.

The permit limitation during each excess emissions event was 96 ppm NOx @ 15% O2, 4-hour average

The events occurred as the following:

December 15, 2022, 20:00 hour; 113.3 ppm NOx @ 15% O2, 4-hour average (17.3 ppm in excess)

Specifically: Unit 8 exceeded NOx @ 15% O2, 4-hour average when CT is operating at &lt; 75% peak load resulting from a shutdown on December 14, 2022. The facility indicated the cause of the event is due to the NOx water injection system not being in operation during the subsequent startup on December 15, 2022. On December 14, 2022, the Unit shut down and a suction pressure alarm prevents the water injection pump from operating. The controls logic for the pump is set up in a way which the NOx water injection pump will not start again until an operator acknowledges and resets the pump controls.

Unit 8 began operation on December 15, 2022 at 17:54 through 21:04. When the Operation's Supervisor notified Plant Management and the Environmental Scientist of Unit 7 exceedance on January 9, 2023, SRP's Air Quality Service discovered the Unit 8 excess emissions event.

The investigation found the data acquisition and handling system issues an exceedance alarm which was acknowledged by the Instruments & Control Specialist without notification to Plant Management and Environmental Staff. 

The facility intends to take the following corrective actions:

Re-configure the controls logic of the NOx water injection pump so it starts up without intervention from an operator
Provide refresher training for Operations on the permit limits and ensure NOx water injection pump is in operation during startup
Implement notification procedure for excess emissions
Implement a daily StackVision review of Alarm History and Downtime Duration Events

Please refer to INSP176407</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT005865</td><td>F000014</td><td>Salt River Project - Agua Fria Generating Station</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP015964</td><td>Salt River Project</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>72-hour Excess Emissions Report for Agua Fria Unit 7. Initial notification made on January 10, 2023.</td><td>01/13/2023</td><td>01/18/2023</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>On January 10, 2023 at 11:56 a.m., the Permitee sent an email notifying the Department of an excess emissions event for Unit 7. The email satisfied Permit Condition 16.c.i. since the deviation was discovered January 9, 2023 during the 11:00 hour. This 72-hour excess emission report also satisfies P.C. 16.c.i.

The permit limitation during each excess emissions event was 96 ppm NOx @ 15% O2, 4-hour average

The events occurred as the following:

December 15, 2022, 20:00 hour; 121.7 ppm NOx @ 15% O2, 4-hour average (25.7 ppm in excess)
December 15, 2022, 21:00 hour; 111.4 ppm NOx @ 15% O2, 4-hour average (15.4 ppm in excess)
January 9, 2023, 11:00 hour; 107.1 ppm NOx @ 15% O2, 4-hour average (11.1 ppm in excess)

Specifically: Unit 7 exceeded NOx @ 15% O2, 4-hour average when CT is operating at &lt; 75% peak load resulting from a shutdown on December 14, 2022. The facility indicated the cause of the event is due to the NOx water injection system not being in operation during the subsequent startups on December 15, 2022 and January 9, 2023. On December 14, 2022, the Unit shut down and a suction pressure alarm prevents the water injection pump from operating. The controls logic for the pump is set up in a way which the NOx water injection pump will not start again until an operator acknowledges and resets the pump controls.

Unit 7 began operation on January 9, 2023 at 11:03 a.m. The pump was brought back online at 11:53 a.m. which reduced the NOx  concentration from 100 ppm to 6 ppm. 

The December 15, 2022 events were later discovered during investigation, which is the last time the Unit was operated before January 9, 2023. The investigation found the data acquisition and handling system issues an exceedance alarm which was acknowledged by the Instruments & Control Specialist without notification to Plant Management and Environmental Staff. 

The facility intends to take the following corrective actions:

Re-configure the controls logic of the NOx water injection pump so it starts up without intervention from an operator
Provide refresher training for Operations on the permit limits and ensure NOx water injection pump is in operation during startup
Implement notification procedure for excess emissions
Implement a daily StackVision review of Alarm History and Downtime Duration Events

Please refer to INSP176407</td></tr>
