<tr><td>CRPT005652</td><td>F000038</td><td>APS West Phx Power Plant</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP003677</td><td>Arizona Public Service Company</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>APS West Phoenix Power Plant 72-hour report for CO startup excess emission that occured on CC5A on 11/14/2022.</td><td>11/15/2022</td><td>11/16/2022</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Specifically: CC5A exceeded CO startup/shutdown 3-hour rolling limit of 870 lb/hr as required by Permit Condition 1.a.ii.1) Table 2a. The excess emissions , which happened during the 1400-1459 hour of November 14, 2022, was recorded by the facility via CEMS/DAHS with a resultant value of 921.5 lb/hr (in excess of 51.5 lb/hr).
I called responsible official, Andre Bodrog, regarding the notice of non-compliance and left a voicemail.**
I sent a preliminary findings of non-compliance email to the responsible official. **

Permit Condition 48.f requires the facility to notify the Maricopa County Air Quality Department within 24 hours of the time when the facility first learned of the excess emissions. The even occurred during the 1400-1459 hour of November 14, 2022, and the Department received the written detailed notification within the appropriate 72-hour timeframe with the necessary information at 11:45 a.m. on November 15, 2022. 
An initial investigation found the excess startup CO emissions from the unit was due to an ignitor malfunction, which led to an abnormal condition. Due to the abnormal startup, the operators were not clear on the startup procedure and did not take the correct action to avoid the excess emission.
Since, APS is resolving the ignitor issue with Siemens and will be working with the involved operators to ensure the startup procedure is fully understood. APS expects this to be an isolated event.

Please refer to INSP174501.</td></tr>
