<tr><td>CRPT005509</td><td>F002663</td><td>Belmark</td><td>Minor</td><td>Printing</td><td>CMP000495</td><td>Belmark Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Permit Close Request</td><td>Permit Cancellation Request received</td><td>10/18/2022</td><td>10/24/2022</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Permit Cancellation Request received via AQDPermits</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT005505</td><td>F000789</td><td>Microchip Technology - Tempe</td><td>Minor</td><td>Semiconductors and Electronics</td><td>CMP002193</td><td>Microchip Technology Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Request for Rule 130 relief for 2 hour bypass of RCTO 20-2 during severe weather event.</td><td>10/18/2022</td><td>11/02/2022</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>This report details the bypass of the RCTO 20-2 unit, on October 15, 2022. On this date, the RCTO 20-2 was bypassed for approximately 2 hours. During this time, the source estimates a total of 14 pounds of VOCs were released to the atmosphere. The source states this bypass was caused by an extreme weather event, which caused a cooling fan pressure fault reading, forcing the unit to bypass mode. The fault was cleared by resetting the equipment, no repairs were required to reengage the RCTO 20-2. 

The source states during their maintenance scheduled for October 18-20, 2022, all actuators, sensors, control units, and any other manufacture supplied equipment will be adjusted to avoid future bypasses caused by weather events. 

I accept this affirmative defense due to the following: 
-	The described cause of this bypass is an emergency, as defined in Rule 130. The source identified the cause as an extreme weather event. 
-	At the time of the emergency the source states the RCTO 20-2 was being properly operated. 
-	The source took all reasonable steps to minimize levels of emissions by following the manufacturer instructions on how to clear such an error. 
-	The source notified the Maricopa County Air Quality Department of this bypass within 24 hours of the occurrence.</td></tr>
