<tr><td>CRPT005373</td><td>F006238</td><td>Durango Service Center</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP002086</td><td>Maricopa County</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Permit Close Request</td><td>Permit Cancellation Request</td><td>09/28/2022</td><td>10/03/2022</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT005371</td><td>F000789</td><td>Microchip Technology - Tempe</td><td>Minor</td><td>Semiconductors and Electronics</td><td>CMP002193</td><td>Microchip Technology Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Notification of Rule 130 emergency bypass of RCTO 20-2 unit.</td><td>09/27/2022</td><td>09/30/2022</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>This report details the bypass of the RCTO 20-2 unit, on September 26, 2022. On this date, the RCTO 20-2 was bypassed for approximately 12 hours. During this time, the source estimates a total of 84 pounds of VOCs were released to the atmosphere. The source states this bypass was caused by a broken motor coupling and shaft keyway, which caused the oxidizer fan to lose pressure, switching the unit to bypass mode. 

The source states long-term wear and corrosion led to the failure of the shaft keyway and motor coupling. The source believes this wear and corrosion was unforeseeable, thus an emergency as defined in Maricopa County Rule 130. The source intends to use this as an affirmative defense against the excess emissions released during this time. 

I accept this affirmative defense due to the following: 

-	The listed cause of this failure was long term wear and corrosion of vital equipment within the RCTO 20-2. It is believed this event was unforeseeable, as no item in the approved O&M plan mentions the shaft keyway, motor coupling, or the greater process in which this equipment is involved in. Furthermore, Mark Large, Facilities Supervisor at Microchip Technology - Tempe, informed me there is no procedure in the maintenance manual provided by Munters, the manufacturer of this RCTO 20-2, to inspect or lubricate the pieces of equipment responsible for this failure. As there is no documented maintenance procedure relating to the shaft keyway, motor coupling, or the process in which these components are involved; from both the Maricopa County approved O&M plan submitted by the source or the manufacturer of this product, it is reasonable to believe this failure was unforeseeable to the source. 
-	At the time of the emergency the source states the RCTO 20-2 was being properly operated. 
-	The source notified the Maricopa County Air Quality Department of this bypass within 24 hours of the occurrence. 

I have instructed this source to add the the oxidizer fan pressure and an inspection for wear and corrosion, of the process in which the shaft keyway and motor coupling are involved in, to the O&M plan.</td></tr>
