<tr><td>CRPT005325</td><td>F000789</td><td>Microchip Technology - Tempe</td><td>Minor</td><td>Semiconductors and Electronics</td><td>CMP002193</td><td>Microchip Technology Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Notification of Rule 130 emergency shut down of VOC thermal oxidizer RCTO 20-2 due to extreme weather. The emergency shut down occurred at approximately 10 pm on Sunday, September 11.</td><td>09/13/2022</td><td>09/21/2022</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>This report details events that occurred on the evening of September 11, 2022. On this date, the RCTO 20-2 unit was bypassed for approximately two hours. During this time, the site estimates a total of 14 pounds of VOCs were released to the atmosphere. The site states, this bypass was conducted due to an emergency, as defined in Maricopa County Rule 130. Specifically, severe weather, an act of God, caused the RCTO 20-2 to register a cooling fan pressure fault which caused the unit to enter emergency shutdown mode, thus bypassing the unit. The site intends to use this emergency as an affirmative defense, against the excess emissions released during this time. 
The site has demonstrated to me this incident was caused by an emergency and the affirmative defense should be granted. The following describes how each section in Rule 130 section 402 have been met: 
-	402.1: Weather reports describe severe weather events in the area of this site, at the time the unit was bypassed.
-	402.1: This event is distinctly different than the two most recent bypass events of the RCTO 20-2. 
o	During this event, the RCTO 20-2 registered a cooling fan pressure fault causing the unit to shut down. The manufacturer instructions were precisely followed to restart this unit. No equipment was damaged, and no maintenance was required to correct this fault. A restart of the unit corrected the fault. 
o	During the two previous events, occurring on May 16 and August 22, 2022, equipment failure or imminent equipment failure led directly to the shutdown and bypass of the RCTO 20-2 unit. For more information regarding these faults and the site’s response please see Compliance Reports: CRPT004896 and CRPT005252 respectively. During these events, maintenance occurred and parts were required to be replaced. 
-	402.2: The site reports the unit was operating normally, prior to the severe weather events. 
-	402.3: The site followed the manufacturer instructions on how to correct this fault, minimizing the amount of VOCs released to the atmosphere. 
o	The site reached out to Dana Eldredge, technical support technician for the RCTO 20-2, to verify the likelihood of this fault being caused by a severe weather event. Dana states this is more than likely the case. 
-	402.4: The site sent me an email on Monday, September 12, 2022 detailing this bypass. This is within the two working day requirement outlined in this section.</td></tr>
