<tr><td>CRPT005297</td><td>F000397</td><td>Exponent</td><td>Minor</td><td>Gasoline Non-resale</td><td>CMP001308</td><td>Exponent of Delaware Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Notice Per 314 section 320/321 subsections 7&8: Performing testing on lithium ion battery on 9/1.
We will be stress testing a lithium-ion battery which, if it catches fire during the testing, would emit less than 2 lbs allowed during a restricted burn period under those rules.  We calculate an emissions factor from a peer reviewed publication (attached).  This is more conservative and less permissive than AP42: Vehicle Parts by a factor of two.  We will be initiating testing in the morning 5-6pm when air quality is improving. 

Battery initial mass: 11.5 (includes non-combustible material)
High end of historical mass loss (e.g. fuel):40%
Emission factor: 11.1%
(initial mass)*(mass loss %)*(emission factor) = 0.5106 pounds of particulate matter</td><td>09/01/2022</td><td>10/24/2022</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Submittal of pre-burn report for testing potential explosive containing products.  Emission calculations confirmed less than 2.0lbs of particulate matter would be emitted if products catch fire during testing.

No noncompliance issues noted.</td></tr>
