<tr><td>CRPT005249</td><td>F000126</td><td>Corrosion Engineering</td><td>Synthetic Minor</td><td>Plastics, Polymers, Fiberglass, Foam</td><td>CMP000947</td><td>Corrosion Engineering Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Periodic Reporting</td><td>Semi-Annual HAPs Report for January-June 2022 Reporting Period</td><td>08/23/2022</td><td>08/25/2022</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Semi-Annual HAPs Report for January - June 2022 received in required timeframe. All requirements of Permit Condition 20 included. No HAP emission exceedances reported. No compliance issues noted.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT004698</td><td>F002454</td><td>L3Harris</td><td>Minor</td><td>Semiconductors and Electronics</td><td>CMP001946</td><td>L-3 Communications Corporation</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>O&M Plan</td><td>L3Harris has been evaluating methods to reduce water consumption.  We intend to reduce the amount of blowdown from the wet scrubbers by installing conductivity controllers.  With these controllers, the scrubbers will only blowdown when a conductivity set point is reached.  

The two newest scrubbers, Scrubbers 98 and 100, were purchased from Viron.  Scott Barton at Viron (989-723-8255) recommended a high conductivity setpoint of 5,000 microSiemens per square centimeter (uS/cm2), meaning the scrubbers can tolerated conductivities at that level.  Conductivity data for all 4 of the scrubbers was collected (see attached).  Scrubbers 98 and 100 had been set to continuously blowdown at 7 gallons per minute (gpm).  At 7 gpm, the conductivity in the recirculating water for both scrubbers was measured at 1,300 to 1,500 uS/cm2.  This indicates that considerably more water than needed is being dumped.  The blowdown rates on Scrubbers 98 and 100 were temporarily set to 3 gpm and then 2 gpm to see how high the conductivity would go.  The highest value measured with either scrubber was 1,630 uS/cm2 , proving that we can still reduce the blowdown rate even further.  

Therefore, the installation of conductivity controllers will be completed for all 4 scrubbers.  Each controller will be set to blowdown conservatively to maintain a conductivity no greater than 4000 uS/cm2.  At this setpoint, makeup and blowdown are initiated.  Revised Operation and Maintenance plans for all four scrubbers will be submitted through ADQImpact.</td><td>08/23/2022</td><td>09/14/2022</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Revised O&M Plans for SEF96, SEF98, SEF100 and SEF102.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT005252</td><td>F000789</td><td>Microchip Technology - Tempe</td><td>Minor</td><td>Semiconductors and Electronics</td><td>CMP002193</td><td>Microchip Technology Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Microchip Tempe has bypassed the RCTO 20-2 unit on August 22, 2022, for 2.4 hours to conduct emergency repairs of the Process Fan #1 instrument tubing. The additional, unabated VOC emissions during the repair are calculated at 17 pounds or 7 pounds per hour.</td><td>08/23/2022</td><td>09/01/2022</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>This report documents the RCTO 20-2 unit was bypassed, on August 22, 2022, for 2.4 hours to conduct repairs per this sites Operation & Maintenance(O&M) Plan. These repairs were conducted on the Process Fan #1 instrument tubing. The site states 17 pounds of VOCs were released to the atmosphere during this operation. This repair was initiated due to the differential pressure dropping from seven inches of water, ultimately to three inches of water. This report and operation are in compliance as the O&M plan was precisely followed during this repair. The parameter responsible for initiating this response was not listed in the O&M plan, however; I requested the site make a revision to their O&M plan to include this and any other parameter vital to the operation of the RCTO 20-2.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT005246</td><td>F002681</td><td>SFPP-Phoenix Terminal</td><td>Title V</td><td>Petroleum Products Bulk Storage/Handling</td><td>CMP002921</td><td>SFPP LP</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Title V Compliance Certification</td><td>2022 Phoenix Seal Gap Report</td><td>08/23/2022</td><td>09/14/2022</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Semi-annual Seal Gap Report for SFPP LP Phoenix Terminal for reporting period 01-Jan-22 to 30-Jun-22. Compliance report was submitted in a timely manner on 23-Aug-22.

