<tr><td>CRPT005036</td><td>F001857</td><td>Rogers Corporation - Price Road Facility</td><td>Title V</td><td>Laminates and Ceramics Manufacturing</td><td>CMP002773</td><td>Rogers Corporation</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Title V Compliance Certification</td><td>Title V Permit Semiannual Compliance Report for the period from 1/1/2022 to 6/30/2022</td><td>07/05/2022</td><td>07/13/2022</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Semiannual monitoring report submitted in accordance with Permit Condition 61: Semiannual Compliance Reporting for NSPS VVV, MACT OOOO and MACT DDDDD. The report was submitted in a timely manner on 05-Jul-22 for the reporting period of 01-Jan-22 to 30-Jun-22. 

Per the report, the facility was not in operation during the reporting period. The report detailed each of the semiannual reporting requirements and confirmed that operations did not occur for the specified equipment. Per my last conversation with Curtis Kempton, Principal Environmental Engineer, the facility does not currently have an expected startup date at this time. All equipment on-site is inoperable, locked out and tagged out, as of last inspection. 

All of the semiannual compliance reporting requirements were met with the submission of this report and was determined to be in compliance.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT005035</td><td>F000041</td><td>APS Ocotillo Power Plant</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP003677</td><td>Arizona Public Service Company</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Submitting the 72-hour Notification for APS Ocotillo GT6 Excess Emission.</td><td>07/03/2022</td><td>07/12/2022</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Compliance report submitted due to excess emissions event on 01-Jul-22 from 2158 to 2159. The 72 hour excess emissions report was submitted in a timely manner on 03-Jul-22. 

Specifically, NOx emissions were reported during the 2100 hour which exceeded the 2.5 ppmvdc Normal Operations Limit and the 9.3 lb/hr NOx limit. Per the report, emissions for the 2100 hour average 3.1 ppmvdc and 10.9 lb/hr respectively. I requested additional documentation to confirm the calculations used to determine the emissions, as well as clarification on the nature of the malfunction.

Chris Chittester, Environmental Section Lead, stated that a PX36 Amplifier malfunctioned, causing the NOx water injection system to fail. Per Mr. Chittester, the resulting emission exceedance was mostly due to the late time in the 2100 hour that the malfunction occurred, which prevented the operator from making adjustments to curb emissions before the end of the hour. The PX36 unit was replaced and APS does not foresee the need for further operational changes in order to prevent future exceedances of this nature.

A Notice of Non-Compliance will be issued for excess emissions. See INSP169685 for further details.</td></tr>
