<tr><td>CRPT004916</td><td>F001759</td><td>Gila River Power Station</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP015964</td><td>Salt River Project</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>72-Hour Excess Emissions Report for May 21, 2022 NOX ppm corrected to 15% O2 had exceeded the 3-hour rolling average limit on Unit 1B.</td><td>05/24/2022</td><td>05/31/2022</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>I reviewed Excess Emissions Report submitted via IMPACT that occurred on May 21, 2022 at 0209hr from Unit 1B Selective Catalytic Reduction (SCR) Inlet NOx analyzer. 

On May 21, 2022, Unit 1B exceeded the 3-hour rolling 2.5 ppm NOx emission limit of permit condition 18.a.iii, Table 5. The maximum value of the exceedance during the event was 2.7 ppm. (The excess emissions was 0.2 ppm).   

Normal maintenance procedures for failed continuous emission monitoring system (CEMS) quality assurance tests require that the CEMS be place in
maintenance mode for troubleshooting and the ammonia injection control valve used to control NOx emissions be place in manual mode to allow operations staff to manually control the flow of ammonia. After correcting the CEMS issue on the NOx inlet analyzer and passing the calibration error test, the CEMS were taken out of maintenance mode and returned to normal operating status, however the ammonia control valve was inadvertently left in manual
operation mode versus normal automatic control by the Distributed Control System (DCS).

At 300, Operations initiated a normal shutdown on Unit 1A while Unit 1B was ramped to a higher load. The increased load on Unit 1B required an increase in ammonia feed to maintain NOx emissions. With the Unit 1B ammonia control valve in manual mode, ammonia feed to the SCR was unable to increase automatically to meet the demand of increasing NOx emissions. The Data Acquisition and Handling System (DAHS) did provide warning alarms for the elevated NOx emissions; however, these were initially misinterpreted as alarms associated with the shutdown of Unit 1A.

At 0401 the ammonia feed was increased but not before the NOx corrected to 15% O2 had exceeded the 3-hour rolling average limit of 2.5 PPM for hour 3:00.

See compliance inspection report ID: INSP168642.</td></tr>
