<tr><td>CRPT004001</td><td>F001644</td><td>Northwest Regional Landfill</td><td>Title V</td><td>Landfills</td><td>CMP003877</td><td>Waste Management Arizona Landfills Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Notification</td><td>As part of a prior NESHAP Notification submitted September 24, 2021, we are providing this letter to updated the dates for the installation of landfill gas wells at the facility.  This work is now set for November 30, 2021 to December 9, 2021.</td><td>11/02/2021</td><td>12/09/2021</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Notification of NESHAP with updated dates for landfill gas well drilling.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT004000</td><td>F000048</td><td>Northwest Water Reclamation Plant</td><td>Minor</td><td>Wastewater Treatment Plants and Lift Stations</td><td>CMP000816</td><td>City of Mesa</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>This is a notification that on 11/1/2021 from 7:55am to 8:00am digester gas was released.  The cogen was in operation but the gas compression shut down due to an alarm.  The flare could not be operated because an emergency bypass system was being installed.  This related to the report submitted for 10/25/2021.  The attached notification describes the event and discharge.</td><td>11/02/2021</td><td>11/04/2021</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>While reviewing the self reported deviation, it has been determined this was a planned maintenance event resulting in an unplanned failure to divert the digester gas to the cogeneration engine.
The cogeneration engine did not fail during the planned maintenance, according to the report, the compressor used to compress the digester gas shut off.

Area of concern:
Permit condition 3.b. states that:
The Permittee may temporarily bypass the boiler and flares for short-term maintenance and route digester gas to the atmosphere while maintaining digester gas operations at the site, as long as the following conditions are met:

i. Such bypass does not cause any emission limit in Permit Condition 1 to be exceeded;
ii. The duration of the maintenance activities shall not to exceed 30 minutes per event and shall not exceed 20 cumulative hours per year;

iii. The Permittee shall keep records in accordance with Permit Condition 4.b to demonstrate
compliance with this Permit Condition; and

iv. The Permittee shall provide written notice to the Control Officer at least 7-days in advance of performing scheduled maintenance on the boiler and flares anytime the maintenance requires the unit(s) to be temporarily bypassed. The notice shall include the date, length of time and purpose for the bypass. Should 20 cumulative hours of bypass be exceeded in any 12-month period, the Permittee shall comply with all record keeping and reporting requirements of Rule 130 (Emergency Provisions) and Rule 140 (Excess Emissions).

According to the report, reasonable efforts were made to prevent the digester gas from venting to the atmosphere and to prevent further events from occurring.</td></tr>
