<tr><td>CRPT003932</td><td>F002126</td><td>Arlington Valley</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP000343</td><td>Arlington Valley LLC</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Combined Cycle System #2 - NOx limit exceedances

Once the excess emissions were confirmed, a phone call was made to Elizabeth Bullard on Wednesday, October 20, 2021, notifying the county of the exceedances. This call was made in the permit required 24-hour window outlined in permit condition 16. f.i.1.

Excess Emissions Report:

1.	The excess emissions occurred out of the exhaust stack associated with GT-2 (Gas Turbine #2)
2.	According to the CEMS, the NOx lb/hr emission limit for Combustion Turbine #2 when duct burners are ON was exceeded for two (2) hours. The lb/hr permit limit is 24.0 lb/hr and the actual emissions were 44.2 lb/hr and 44.0 lb/hr. Likewise, the NOx 3-hour rolling average limit of 2.5 ppm was exceeded for six (6) 3-hour averages. The six averages were 2.6 ppm, 2.8 ppm, 3.9 ppm, 4.8 ppm, 4.5 ppm, and 3.4 ppm.
3.	The NOx limit of 24.0 lb/hr was exceeded for the hours of 18 and 19 on 9/1/2021.  The NOx 2.5 ppm limit was exceeded for the 3-hour averages associated with hours 16, 17, 18, 19, 20, and 21 on 9/1/2021.
4.	The emissions were emitted from the equipment associated with CEMS 2 (GT-2)
5.	Upon completion of the routine monthly QA checklist associated with CEMS 2, the technician forgot to switch the CEMS out of maintenance/calibration mode and back into the normal operating mode. The QA checklist was finished at 13:36. CEMS 2 was left in maintenance mode from 13:36 to shortly after 20:00 so roughly 7 hours. While the CEMS unit is in maintenance/calibration mode, the control system automatically keeps the ammonia flow control valve in the same position. During the afternoon GT2 ramped up in load while also firing duct burners. Since the ammonia flow control valve was kept in the same position it was not able to automatically adjust for the increased load throughout the day. This lack of adjustment lead to not enough ammonia being injected and resulted in the excess emissions.
6.	The excess emissions were not emitted due to an equipment malfunction.
7.	Once the nightshift control room operator noticed that CEMS 2 was not in the correct operating mode, a phone call was made to the technician who performed the routine maintenance, and he walked the CRO though putting CEMS 2 back into the correct mode. Once this change was made the ammonia control valve went back into Auto and adjustments were made to lower the NOx emissions to normal levels.
8.	The excess emissions were not a result of startup or malfunction.</td><td>10/22/2021</td><td>11/10/2021</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Combined Cycle System #2 - NOx limit exceedances. I received a phone call from Tyler Burrell on 20-Oct-21 informing me of the discovery of the NOx 3-hour exceedance. This is a self-report violation and will be reviewed by enforcement.</td></tr>
