<tr><td>CRPT003815</td><td>F001461</td><td>Cave Creek Landfill</td><td>Minor</td><td>Soil Remediation Unit, Portable</td><td>CMP002086</td><td>Maricopa County</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Periodic Reporting</td><td>Q3 Quarterly Performance Report. Due on September 24, 2021, 30 days following the sampling event on August 25, 2021. This report is being submitted one week later than originally scheduled due to a 1 week delay in the sampling event. The county was notified of these sampling dates in advance.</td><td>09/24/2021</td><td>10/21/2021</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Performance Test conducted 25-Aug-21 for the third quarter of 2021. 

Test conducted by Wood Environment & Infrastructure Solutions, Inc., with additional lab reporting by AirTech Environmental Laboratories. Test was submitted on 24-Sep-21, within 30 days of test completion.


PERMIT CONDITIONS

PC1) Allowable Emissions: 
a.) Per report, 12-month rolling emissions totals are as follows:
- VOC: 117.1lbs, limit: 6,397lbs 
- HAPs: 876lbs, limit: 6,979lbs

PC7) Vapor Sampling Requirements:

a.) The following information was gathered as part of the soil vapor emissions (SVE) control unit test:
- i.) SVE samples were taken from the SVE-03 and SVE-04 wells, as wells from Skid 1 and Skid 2
- ii.) Influent VOC concentration was determined to be 46.4mg/m3 (9.8ppmv) with effluent VOC concentration estimated to be 0.103mg/m3 (0.03ppmv). VOC removal efficiency determined to be 99.8%. Influent HAP concentration was determined to be 69mg/m3 (15.9ppmv), with effluent HAP concentration of 0.2mg/m3 (0.1ppmv). HAP removal efficiency determined to be 99.7%.
- iii.) Volumetric flow rate measured to be 925 ft3/min at time of testing.
c.iii.) Process temperature of 102 degrees Fahrenheit at time of testing.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT003817</td><td>F002126</td><td>Arlington Valley</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP000343</td><td>Arlington Valley LLC</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Combined Cycle System #1 SCR malfunction - NOx limit exceedance

A phone call was made to Steve Depenbrok on Thursday, September 23, 2021, notifying the county of the SCR malfunction. This call was made in the permit required 24-hour window outlined in permit condition 16. f.i.1.

Excess emissions report:
1.	The excess emissions are associated with the GT-1 (Turbine 1) exhaust stack.
2.	The BACT 3-hour rolling average limit for NOx is 2.5 ppm for each Combined Cycle System exhaust, per Table 5 of the TV permit
3.	The 3-hour rolling average limit was exceeded on GT-1 exhaust for the hour of 20:00 on 9/22/21.
4.	Excess emissions resulted from the malfunction of dilution air flow fan Alpha that is associated with the SCR for GT-1.
5.	Upon investigation, technician found a motor termination lead in the junction box had overheated and grounded which tripped the motor to the fan.
6.	The wire was cut back, re-lugged and bolted and then taped. The motor returned to service on 9/23/2021 around 09:30
7.	The Control Room Operator (CRO) switched to the Bravo dilution air flow fan/heater once the Alpha fan malfunctioned. 
8.	No other permit procedures need to be addressed based on this incident.</td><td>09/24/2021</td><td>09/30/2021</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Combined Cycle System #1 SCR malfunction - NOx limit exceedance. Notice of non-compliance issued on 30-Sep-21. 

Exceedance of 2.5ppm 3-hour rolling average

Per 40 CFR 60.332(a)(1): “ No owner or operator subject to the provisions of this subpart shall cause to be discharged into the atmosphere from any stationary gas turbine, any gases which contain nitrogen oxides in excess of 2.5ppm NOx.”

As stated in the compliance report the malfunction was addressed and corrected immediately.</td></tr>
