<tr><td>CRPT003664</td><td>F002813</td><td>Calgon Carbon Corporation</td><td>Minor</td><td>Chemical Manufacture, Processing, etc</td><td>CMP000654</td><td>Calgon Carbon Corporation</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Semi-annual CAP Report</td><td>08/17/2021</td><td>08/20/2021</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Semi-annual Corrective Action Plan report (CAP) received on 17-Aug-2021 and reviewed on 20-Aug-2021.

Source submitted four (4) malfunction reports during the time period of 01-Jan-2021 through 30-Jun-2021.

23-Feb-2021
Transmitter malfunction
TO temperature fell below permit required level of 1400 degrees Fahrenheit, which caused the
bypass stack to open. The maintenance team identified the draft transmitter for the thermal oxidizer was not
operating properly, which caused it to provide false draft reading that caused draft to rise to a higher than optimal
level. The positive draft reduced heat being pulled into the TO and allowed the temperature to drop below
permitted level. As a corrective action, the transmitter was replaced, and calibration equipment was purchased
to test the accuracy of the transmitter. 

26-Mar-2021
Fuse malfunction
A fuse that supplied a signal from the Thermal Oxidizer emergency stack damper to the PLC blew.
When the PLC lost the signal, all burners shut down and both Kiln Bypass stacks opened. Maintenance was called
in and diagnosed the issue. Once found, it was noted that there was no indication of a short or significant electrical surge to cause the blown
fuse. The fuse had degraded due to age and exposure to heat.
As a corrective action, the maintenance team created a list of fuses for all panels, replaced them during the outage,
and amended panel PMs to create a schedule for periodic replacement. 

15-Apr-2021
Sensor malfunction
The flame for #2 Kiln went out at 21:46. This shut off the feed screw. After checking systems, the
process to re-light the kiln started at 22:17. This caused the Thermal Oxidizer to heat up from excess air coming
from the kiln. The control system cut gas from the T.O. burner to compensate. When the Kiln flame lit at 22:28,
there was no longer excess air heating the T.O. and the flame was too low to maintain adequate temperature.
Typically, we would manually raise the burner on the T.O. to compensate. This was not done and resulted in the
malfunction. The maintenance tech identified the cause of the Kiln flameout as a dirty fire eye. As a correction
action the maintenance team cleaned the fire eye and changed PMs to inspect them more often. Additional
training was given to the operator on shift to further instruct him on what the process should have been for relighting the kiln after flameout.

30-Jun-2021
Power outage
There was a thunderstorm that caused a power outage. To be proactive in reducing emissions
during storm related power outages, operators observe radar when storms are expected and shut feed off ahead
of a storm to avoid or at least limit emissions.</td></tr>
