<tr><td>CRPT003105</td><td>F002813</td><td>Calgon Carbon Corporation</td><td>Minor</td><td>Chemical Manufacture, Processing, etc</td><td>CMP000654</td><td>Calgon Carbon Corporation</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Permit expires on 31-Oct-2021. A Non-Title V permit renewal application shall be submitted 6 to 18 months prior to the expiration date.

Annual administrative fees received on 15-Sep-2020.

Routine inspection conducted on 12-Apr-2019.

Malfunction notification and report received by Maricopa County Air Quality Department within acceptable time frame.

Stack/emission point: The emissions occurred at the #2 bypass stack.

Date and duration: The event occurred on 15-Apr-2021, beginning at 2228 hours and ending on or before 2230 hours.

Equipment involved: The emissions emanated from #2 kiln to #2 bypass.

Nature and cause of malfunction: The malfunction occurred when the flame for #2 kiln went out at 2146 hours. This in turn shut off the feed screw. The process to re-light the kiln began at 2217 hours. This caused the thermal oxidizer to heat up from excess air originating from the kiln. The control system cut gas from the thermal oxidizer burner to compensate. The kiln flame was relit at 2228 hours and there was no longer excess air heating the thermal oxidizer. The flame was too low to maintain an adequate temperate. Typically source would manually raise the burner on the thermal oxidizer to compensate but this was not done and the malfunction occurred.

Corrective action taken: The feed was stopped by interlock. The technician identified the cause of the kiln flameout as a dirty fire eye. This item was cleaned and will be inspected on a more frequent basis. Additional training will be given to the operator on process for re-lighting the kiln if flameout occurs. 

Emissions: Per the source the malfunction did not cause the facility to exceed permit limits (calculations provided based on 2016 stack testing). Note that future malfunction reports will be required to utilize calculations based on 2021 stack testing.

The calculations assume the duration of the malfunction is from when the stack cap opens to when it is closed and the pollution control system is fully operational or resident time in the reactivation kiln is approximately two hours, thus emissions associated with a malfunction will be limited to a max duration of two hours, whichever is less.

The source is to include malfunction emissions in the rolling total emissions report.

Emissions due to the malfunction event:
NOX – 0.00 lbs 
CO –  0.07 lbs
VOC – 0.00 lbs
PM – 0.03 lbs
HCl – 0.00 lbs</td><td>04/17/2021</td><td>04/21/2021</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Malfunction event occurred on 15-Apr-2021
Malfunction notification received on 16-Apr-2021
Malfunction report received on 17-Apr-2021
Malfunction report reviewed and accepted on 21-Apr-2021</td></tr>
