<tr><td>CRPT002473</td><td>F001332</td><td>Caljet</td><td>Synthetic Minor</td><td>Petroleum Products Bulk Storage/Handling</td><td>CMP015461</td><td>Caljet of America LLC</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Periodic Reporting</td><td>This submittal is a semi-annual compliance report covering the period of July 1, 2020, through December 31, 2020. The submittal is due by Jan 31.</td><td>01/18/2021</td><td>02/10/2021</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Report includes the following required information: 

List of storage tanks on site (confirmed)
Loading rack vapor tightness certification (confirmed) 
Equipment Leak Inspection: No leaks that were not repaired within 15 days of detection (confirmed)
Excess Emissions: No excess emissions during reporting period (confirmed) 
Calibration Gas Expiration Data. 

CEMS Quarterly CGA Reports included as well. 

Excess inaccuracy reported. From July 1st through September 22nd 2020. Corrective action taken explained as follows: 

The CEMS analyzer did not pass the EPA Performance Specification (PS) 8A 7-Day drift test which was performed on
June 25, 2020. The analyzer appears to be experiencing a high bias reading on the daily drift readings for the Span
concentrations. Caljet of America LLC worked with the CEMS analyzer manufacturer (IR Industries) as well as Jordan
Technologies (third-party PMI contractor) to correct the high bias readings. It was determined that the CEMS
analyzer was operating correctly and the issues were identified to be due to a faulty gas regulator as well as check
valves along the sample train. The parts were repaired and corrected on August 6, 2020, and a EPA Performance
Specification (PS) 8A Re-Test was performed on September 22, 2020. The results of the re-test indicated that the
CEMS unit and sample train were deemed to be back in control.

No compliance issues observed.</td></tr>
