<tr><td>CRPT002057</td><td>F000013</td><td>Salt River Project - Santan Generating Station</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP015964</td><td>Salt River Project</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Periodic Reporting</td><td>2020 B SA Certification and Compliance Report April 2020 - September 2020</td><td>10/28/2020</td><td>11/06/2020</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Semi Annual Monitoring Report and Compliance Certification For F000013 - San Tan Generating Station was dated October 28, 2020, and was submitted for the reporting period April 1, 2020 to September 30, 2020.

This report is required by Permit Conditions 4.b. and 21.a. 

In summary of Permit Condition 21. a.
The Monitoring Report and the Compliance Certification were each signed with a certification statement.
1. The summary of compliance status was included in the report for each Permit Condition numbers 1 - 41. Permit Conditions 19.j.ii.4, 19.j.iii.2, and 20.f.ii each indicated intermittent compliance status (violations were issued for these items on May 14, 2020). All other Permit Conditions indicated continuous compliance status.
2. No deviations were reported for the reporting period April 1, 2020 to September 30, 2020.  
3. Summary of hours for the combustion turbine and duct burner hours were reported.
4. No visible emissions were reported and documentation was included.
5. One odor complaint for fish smell was received on April 1, 2020.
6. According to the report, there was insufficient data to determine the 12-month rolling total for NOx emissions during duct burner operating days.
7. San Tan only receives ultra low sulfur diesel for use in the emergency engines and emergency fire pumps.
8. The monthly and 12-month rolling total of the amount of unleaded gasoline delivered. The maximum throughput (PC 19.j.) is 120,000 gallons per any 12 consecutive calendar months, and the total amount of gasoline received was 1,736 gallons.
9. According to the report, the fill pipe was not removed and re-installed during the reporting period. Therefore, no installation inspections were performed. During the annual routine inspection on August 18, 2020, the fill pipe was measured three times and the greatest maximum reading was 3.0 inches (63.0 inches - 60.0 inches) from the bottom of the fill pipe to the bottom of the tank.
10. No additional reporting requirements associated with support operations were recorded.
11. The 40CFR Part 60 report was submitted for April 2020 - September 2020, and other records required by the permit are maintained on site and can be made available upon request.

I sent a static copy of the Inspection Report to Tom Murray, Mark Landers and Lori Trout.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT002058</td><td>F000013</td><td>Salt River Project - Santan Generating Station</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP015964</td><td>Salt River Project</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Periodic Reporting</td><td>2020 S2 Part 60 Report April 2020 - September 2020</td><td>10/28/2020</td><td>11/06/2020</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>40CFR Part 60 Subpart Da and Subpart GG Excess Emissions and Monitoring Systems Performance Report San Tan Generating Station was dated October 28, 2020, and was submitted for the reporting period April 1, 2020 to September 30, 2020.

The report indicates that there were no excess emissions during the reporting period. 

The report is required by Permit Condition 21.d. and 21.e.

Permit Condition 21.d. If the Permittee elects to continuously monitor NOx emissions under 40 CFR Part 60, Subpart GG, the Permittee shall submit reports of excess emissions and monitor downtime, in accordance with 40 CFR §60.7(c). Excess emissions shall be reported for all periods of unit operation, including startup, shutdown, and malfunction. For the purposes of reports required under 40 CFR §60.7(c), periods of excess emissions and monitor downtime that shall be reported are defined as follows:

i. An hour of excess emissions shall be any unit operating hour in which the 4-hour rolling average NOx concentration exceeds the applicable emission limit in Permit Condition 18.b.ii.1). For the purposes of this subpart, a “4-hour rolling average NOx concentration” is the arithmetic average of the average NOx concentration measured by the CEMS for a given hour (corrected to 15 percent oxygen and, if required under 40 CFR §60.335(b) (1), to ISO standard conditions) and the three unit operating hour average NOx concentrations immediately preceding that unit operating hour.

ii. A period of monitor downtime shall be any unit operating hour in which sufficient data are not obtained to validate the hour, for either NOx concentration or diluent (or both).

iii. Each report shall include the ambient conditions (temperature, pressure, and humidity) at the time of the excess emission period. The Permittee does not have to report ambient conditions if they opt to use the worst case ISO correction factor as specified in 40 CFR §60.334(b)(3)(ii), or if the Permittee is not using the ISO correction equation under the provisions of 40 CFR §60.335(b)(1).

Permit Condition 21.e. All reports required under 40 CFR §60.7(c) shall be postmarked by the 30th day following the end of each 6-month period, or shall be submitted with the semiannual compliance reports, as approved by the Control Officer.

I sent this Inspection Report to Tom Murray, Mark Landers and Lori Trout via email.</td></tr>
