<tr><td>CRPT001778</td><td>F002813</td><td>Calgon Carbon Corporation</td><td>Minor</td><td>Chemical Manufacture, Processing, etc</td><td>CMP000654</td><td>Calgon Carbon Corporation</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Permit expires on 31-Oct-2021. A Non-Title V permit renewal application shall be submitted 6 to 18 months prior to the expiration date.

Annual administrative fees received on 25-Sep-2019.

Routine inspection conducted on 12-Apr-2019.

Malfunction notification and report received by Maricopa County Air Quality Department within acceptable time frame.

Stack/emission point: Emissions occurred at the #1 and #2 bypass stacks

Date and duration: Event occurred on 10-Sep-2020 beginning at 0000 hours and ending on or before 0200 hours

Equipment involved: #1 kiln to #1 bypass and #2 kiln to #2 bypass

Nature and cause of malfunction: The air dryer became blocked and stopped air flow from the plant. This reduced the air pressure in the plant, causing the plant burners to shut down due to a safety interlock. The feed was stopped by interlock.

Corrective action taken: Maintenance technician identified the issue and bypassed the air dryer. The air compressor vendor was contacted and is scheduled to service the air dryer. 

Emissions: Per the source the malfunction did not cause the facility to exceed permit limits (calculations provided based on 2016 stack testing).

The calculations assume the duration of the malfunction is from when the stack cap opens to when it is closed and the pollution control system is fully operational or resident time in the reactivation kiln is approximately two hours, thus emissions associated with a malfunction will be limited to a max duration of two hours, whichever is less.
The source is to include malfunction emissions in the rolling total emissions report.

Emissions due to the malfunction event:
NOX – 0.03 lbs
CO – 8.03 lbs
VOC – 0.01 lbs
PM – 3.00 lbs
HCl – 0.04 lbs</td><td>09/14/2020</td><td>09/14/2020</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Malfunction report to equipment failure</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT001777</td><td>F000011</td><td>Salt River Project - East Valley Service Center</td><td>Minor</td><td>Electrical Power Distribution</td><td>CMP015964</td><td>Salt River Project</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Notification</td><td>East Valley Service Center Notification of Non-Compliance.</td><td>09/10/2020</td><td>10/20/2020</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>I reviewed the Non-Compliance Event Self-Report, SRP – East Valley Service Center, Non-Title V Permit # P0006195, which was dated September 9, 2020. After research and discussion with Todd Martin and Eric Poole, it was decided that the source will not be issued a notice of non-compliance under Rule 220 Section 406.3.a.1. The above ground gasoline storage tank was put into operation on September 10, 2020, and the minor modification was submitted on September 3, 2020. The results were no adverse impact to air quality.  

The current Permit Conditions (PC) contains a throughput limit (PC 18.) and also contains Rules 352 and 353 (Permit Conditions 20, 21, 23, 24, 25, 26, 28, and 29). Permit Condition 22 refers to below ground gasoline storage tanks. The Permit Conditions will be changed to incorporate above ground gasoline storage tanks with the submittal of the Minor Modification on September 3, 2020.</td></tr>
