<tr><td>CRPT001744</td><td>F001759</td><td>Gila River Power Station</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP015964</td><td>Salt River Project</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>72-Hour Excess Emissions Report for the Unit 4A excess emissions event on September 4, 2020.</td><td>09/04/2020</td><td>09/08/2020</td><td>No</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>September 4, 2020:

At approximately, 1:00 PM, I received an e-mail from Roy Campbell of Gila River Power Station (GRPS), reporting excess carbon monoxide (CO) start up emissions on Unit 4A, on at 0500 on September 4, 2020. Mr. Campbell informed me in his e-mail, this incident was under investigation and he would send a report to me, once they know what caused this exceedance.
Mr. Campbell did not report the magnitude of the exceedance in his e-mail, but he did inform me the incident was under investigation and would submit a written report once the cause is known.

Mr. Campbell submitted the 72-hour Excess Emissions Report on September 4, 2020. According to the report, the exceedance was a result of an Operations Specialist failure to execute steps required for a “cold start.” The Operations Specialist failure to follow these steps resulted in an extended time period for startup, and high CO emissions. 

Startup Emissions Exceedance:
Identity of Emission Point: Unit 4A
Magnitude of Excess Emission: 229.3 lb/hr, CO Startup 
Permit Limitation: 212 lb/hr, CO Startup Limit (1-hour average)
Duration of Emission: 1 hour, 9/4/2020 0500-0559

Corrective actions described in the report will include one on one coaching and training all plant Operations Specialists including the Operations Specialist involved in this event to use proper startup practices during a cold start up. In addition to procedures for responding to CEMS warning alarms.

September 8, 2020: 
After review of the 72-hour Excess Emissions Report, I observed the report only provided the hour of the event. I sent Mr. Campbell an e-mail to request an additional report providing the emissions before and after the one-hour event reported.

Mr. Campbell sent me the Average Startup for CO Emissions Report for September 4, 2020.
I called Mr. Campbell to discuss this report and he explained Unit 4A was in normal operation mode at 0545. Mr. Campbell also provided the Operation Specialists explanation of the event.
The Operations Specialist attempted to ramp the unit to avoid the exceedance.
Mr. Campbell explained, if the Operations Specialist had shut the unit down instead of attempting to ramp, they might have avoided the excess emissions event.


September 9, 2020:
I sent Mr. Campbell an e-mail with the preliminary findings to notify him I would issue a Notice of Non-Compliance for exceeding the allowable emission limit for Startup CO per Permit Condition 18.A. Table 4.

In Violation of the following:
1.240 § 305.1: In addition to emission limits expressed elsewhere in this Permit, the Permittee shall not cause, allow, or permit emissions to exceed the limits shown in Tables 4</td></tr>
