<tr><td>CRPT001452</td><td>F002504</td><td>Solana Generating Station</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP000333</td><td>Arizona Solar One LLC</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Periodic Reporting</td><td>Title V Report (Semi-annual Report for January – June 2020)</td><td>07/23/2020</td><td>08/03/2020</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Title V Report (Semi-annual Report for January – June 2020)
Arizona Solar One LLC, Permit V17001, Facility ID F002504. issued January 23, 2019
This report covers the period between January 1, 2020 and June 30, 2020

Important Points: 


During January -June 2020
Leak registry: 3433 ball joints registered, Graded 1/2/3 leaking

Repairs/Repacking: 557 ball joints repacked (Note that no repacking occurs during outage times and we catch up on repacking due in the last 6 months of the year. In addition, our work hours were reduced and strict schedules were implemented due to COVID19, resulting in reduced time for maintenance efforts.



The number of valves, pumps, flanges/connectors, ball joints, pressure relief valves and any U/L joints for which leaks were not repaired, identifying the number of those that are determined nonrepairable;

552 ball joints (3% total ball joints) scheduled to be repacked; 0 are nonrepairable.

The power block data is summarized in the table below; note that a solar field collector leak and evac truck leak are also included in the list. All were repaired and closed, except 5, which are currently in the maintenance process and the repairs have not been delayed. (Chart located on records that are attached)

The facts that explain any delay of repairs and, where appropriate, why a process unit/collector loop shutdown was technically infeasible;

Ball Joints are located in operable loops containing at 20 ball joints. It is contractually infeasible to shut down each loop where a ball joint is identified leaking due to the APS energy provision agreement and Department of Energy contract. We track each leaking ball joint by emission grade, as identified in the LDAR Plan. Loops are prioritized for shut down and repair based on the number of ball joints leaking and severity of the leaks. Note that any Grade 1 leak is immediately isolated and that loop is taken out of service until a repacking or repair can be completed.



Ball Joint Survey Results (24-hour Semi-annual Survey conducted April 15-16, 2020) - see data on records attachment.

No violations observed.</td></tr>
