<tr><td>CRPT001319</td><td>F002504</td><td>Solana Generating Station</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP000333</td><td>Arizona Solar One LLC</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>FILE REVIEW:
- File Review Began: 6/29/2020
- Source Classification: Title V
- Last Routine Inspection: Conducted on 12/30/2019
- Violation History: Title V 12/30/2019, 2/25/2019, 8/28/2018 
(Last 2 years for synthetic minor permit #080073): 9/10/2018, 11/28/2017, 8/23/2017, 7/25/2017
- Fees Paid: Yes 
- Recent Complaints: 6/01/2020 see CMPL003269
- Contact up to date 


Timeline of events:

-On 6/29/2020 a notification of excess emissions was received via email by the Air Quality Department. Carrolette Winstead the Quality & Environmental Manager of Arizona Solar One reported the following:

 “We are writing to report that we had an emissions release yesterday that we believe exceeded Condition 3 of our permit (HTF &gt;40 lbs). We are currently allowing the area cool so that we can investigate the cause. Within the next couple of days, we will evaluate the HTF balance in the plant and provide an estimate of the total amount of HTF lost.”
This email satisfies the twenty four hour notice requirement pursuant to permit condition 66.f.i.1. I informed Carrolette that a detailed written report of the excess emissions must be submitted within 72 hours. She acknowledged. 

-On 7/2/2020 Rule 140 response and full excess emissions report was received. The leak was first observed at 7:45 AM on 6/28/2020. The leak continued until 12:00 PM 7/1/2020. The vapor leak occurred in an area near the field main pump suction header crossover high point vent. Once the leak was identified the facility began to bring the plant offline. Shutdown was completed within 1 hour of leak identification. 

The cause of the release was identified as the following: 

“We determined the cause of the release is attributable to a weld failure where the 2” high point vent line is connected to the 42” suction header (Pictures 2 and 3). This line is located ~10 meters in the air without regular access and required scaffolding to access the area.”

It is important to note that this weld failure is adjacent to where the last 2” weld failed on 8/28/2018. Additionally, a second weld failure of this type occurred on 2/25/2019.  Both resulting in excess emission violations. With this new event, three weld failures have resulted in plant leaks. The facility inspected the welds thoroughly in 2019. The inspection revealed that the welds were in operable condition and there were no indications of any issue.

Resulting Emissions: 
Due to the line leak approximately 7.6 tons of Heat Transfer Fluid (HTF) was lost. 50 gallons (.22 tons) of HTF was liquid and spilled into the soil below. The total amount of reported release becomes 7.38 tons HTF lost. This equates to 7.38 tons of VOC emitted. The HTF consists of 26.5-27% Biphenyl which has been identified as a Hazardous Air Pollutant (HAP). This indicates that within the 7.38 tons of VOC, 1.95 tons of a single HAP (Biphenyl) was emitted. The leak caused a total of 7.38 tons of VOC with 1.95 tons of HAP to be released into the atmosphere. 

**Notice of Non-Compliance Issued**

Issued based off the following permit condition 3:

Operation Limitations:
Non-Complying Solvents (HTF): No person shall discharge more than 40 pounds (18 kg) of volatile organic compounds into the atmosphere in
any one day from any machine, equipment, device or other article for employing, applying, evaporating or drying any non-complying solvent (as
defined in Section 202 of MCAQD Rule 330).

Corrective Actions: 
The welds were repaired on 7/2/2020. In order to mitigate future stress on the welds and vent lines an expansion loop has been installed. This was completed on 7/2/2020. Once these repairs were completed the welding inspector completed a magnetic particle test on all welds to confirm weld integrity. These actions correct the violation. 

Affirmative Defense claimed please see Excess Emissions Report with Rule 140 Response attachment for further details.</td><td>06/29/2020</td><td>07/02/2020</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Excess Emissions Self Report & Affirmative Defense</td></tr>
