Report Submittal due within 60 days of Optimization - i.e. 24Nov2019.
 Current report:          Submitted: 01Jul2020; for period 27-Jul-2019 to 23Sep2019 after 92,217.15 tons HMA produced.</td><td>07/01/2020</td><td>09/15/2020</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>020051 VMC Burner Optimization Report for period 27-Jul-2019 to 23-Sep-2019 [Due every 200,000 tons]
• Optimization conducted 23-25 Sep-2019
• Submitted: 01-Jul-2020

Per 14Sep2020 email from Brian Chronowski; HMA Production between 27-Jul-2019 and 25-Sep-2019 is 95,217 tons.
Flame Analyzer conducted on 27Jul2019 and 25Sep2019 [Due every 100,000 tons]

Late submittal: Due 24-Nov-2019; Submitted 01-Jul-2020. Per conference call with Scott Johnson and Brian Chronowski; the period this occurred has been a tumultuous time in the order of personnel changes; as the entire compliance team has turned over.  Mr. Johnson says this an effort will be made to ensure this does not recur.  Per source logs, Sydney Stauffer logged submittal of this report on 24-Nov-2019. Unable to locate in Department files.

On 15-Sep-2020; I telephoned Mr. Chronowski and informed him of the following Area of Concern:

AREA OF CONCERN: [Rule 220 §302.2; p.c. 2.d.]: which, in summary, states; Permittee shall submit a combustion optimization report to the control officer within 60 days.
• Department has no record of receipt of subject report, until 01-Jul-2020.
• Mr. Johnson explained source logs show Sydney Stauffer submitted report to MCAQD on 24-Nov-2019.

I informed Mr. Chronowski a violation may be issued for recurrence.

No violations noted.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT001292</td><td>F002813</td><td>Calgon Carbon Corporation</td><td>Minor</td><td>Chemical Manufacture, Processing, etc</td><td>CMP000654</td><td>Calgon Carbon Corporation</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Permit expires on 31-Oct-2021. A Non-Title V permit renewal application shall be submitted 6 to 18 months prior to the expiration date.

Annual administrative fees received on 25-Sep-2019.

Routine inspection conducted on 12-Apr-2019.

Malfunction notification and report received by Maricopa County Air Quality Department within acceptable time frame.

Stack/emission point: Emissions occurred at the #2 bypass stack.
Date and duration: Event occurred on 21-Jun-2020, beginning at 1941 hours and ending on or before 2141 hours.
Equipment involved: Emissions emanated from #2 kiln to #2 bypass.
Nature and cause of malfunction: A variable frequency drive for the main combustion blower failed, thereby removing power from the device. The device is required to run all of the plant burners and they shut down without it. Without burners for the thermal oxidizer, the bypass stack opened.
Corrective action taken: The feed was stopped by interlock. Maintenance department identified the failed variable frequency drive and a replacement part was ordered. Plant remains not in operation due to not having a spare on site. Additional spare device ordered by the source to prevent lengthy outages in the future.
Emissions: Per the source the malfunction did not cause the facility to exceed permit limits (calculations provided based on 2016 stack testing).

The calculations assume the duration of the malfunction is from when the stack cap opens to when it is closed and the pollution control system is fully operational or resident time in the reactivation kiln is approximately two hours, thus emissions associated with a malfunction will be limited to a max duration of two hours, whichever is less.

The source is to include malfunction emissions in the rolling total emissions report.

Emissions due to the malfunction event (in pounds):
NOX – 0.02
CO – 4.02
VOC – 0
PM – 1.50
HCl – 0.02

No violations issued due to this malfunction.

Items used to determine compliance:
Malfunction notification
Malfunction report

