<tr><td>CRPT001280</td><td>F001759</td><td>Gila River Power Station</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP015964</td><td>Salt River Project</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Self reported excess emissions for CO during Start Up.</td><td>06/18/2020</td><td>06/29/2020</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>June 18, 2020:
I received an e-mail from Roy Campbell, the Senior Engineer for the Gila River Power Station, to report an exceedance of Carbon Monoxide (CO) emissions during start up on June 17, 2020.
Mr. Campbell provided a screen shot of the alarm received by personnel in the control room.

June 19, 2020
Sent me an e-mail with the recording of the minute data for the CO emissions during start up.
Mr. Campbell explained in his e-mail, the software used at the plant had miscalculated the CO emissions by "using the hourly lbs/MMBtu average and multiplying by the MMBtu average heat input for the hour rather than just summing the lbs for each minute of the startup event."

According to the data provided by Mr. Campbell showing the CO emissions for June 17, 2020, the emissions during start up were 166.8 lbs/hr. and the limit is 212 lbs/hr.
Mr. Campbell also informed me they are working to correct the software calculation method.

No compliance issues observed.</td></tr>
