<tr><td>CRPT001235</td><td>F000055</td><td>Greenfield Water Reclamation Plant</td><td>Minor</td><td>Wastewater Treatment Plants and Lift Stations</td><td>CMP000816</td><td>City of Mesa</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Digester Gas Excess Sulfur Content Report for  Greenfield Water Reclamation Plant, emailed June 15, 2020</td><td>06/15/2020</td><td>06/29/2020</td><td>No</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>June 15, 2020:
Jen Hetherington, the Regulatory Compliance Program Manager for the City of Mesa, Greenfield Water Reclamation Plant, sent an e-mail to the Department to report the Greenfield Water Reclamation Plant had a exceeded the allowable Digester sulfur content of 310ppmv on June 10, 2020. Ms Hetherington provided the Digester Sulfur Content Report for 2020.
According to the report the sulfur content on June 10, 2020 was 501ppmv for Digester 1 and 470ppmv for Digester 2.
June 18, 2020:
Ms Hetherington sent an e-mail with a possible explanation from the Plant Supervisor, for the high sulfur content. According to the Plant Supervisor, the high sulfur content may have been caused by the rate at which they have had to “feed” the digesters from the digested sludge tank.
They had to remove the digested sludge tank from operation for about 2 months and prior to this it was a continuous feed of the digested sludge to the digesters every 24 hours.
When the digested sludge tank was taken out of service they had to change the “feeding” pattern to a rate that equaled 2-3 times the normal rate. The difference in the rate of digested sludge being fed to the digester may have resulted in a biological change affecting the H2S in the system.
According to Ms. Hetherington, the sludge storage tank was put back into service on June 15, 2020.  The sulfur content of the Digesters was analyzed daily and the sulfur content has been reduced to below the allowable limt.  I expect the gas taken today to be back under the permit limit. 
June 23, 2020:
Ms. Hetherington submitted the Digester Sulfur Content report showing the sulfur content for Digester 1 and were below 310ppmv. written report required within 72 hours of the occurrence of the exceedance event. The report showed the sulfur content remained over 310ppmv from June 10, 2020 until June 22. According to the report samples were takin for Digester 2 on June 18th, however samples could not be taken for Digester 1. Digester 2 had a sulfur content of 288ppmv, on June 22md both Digesters had a sulfur content below 310ppmv.
July 1, 2020:
I called Ms. Hetherington and informed her, I would issue a notice of non-compliance for exceeding the allowable sulfur content limit required by Permit Condition and Rule 220 § 302.2: The Permittee shall not burn any digester gas in the boilers or flares that contains sulfur in excess of 310 parts per million by volume (ppmv).

I also informed Ms. Hetherington by e-mail, of the pending violation for exceeding the allowable sulfur content required in Permit Condition 2 and Rule 220 § 302.2.</td></tr>
