<tr><td>CRPT001182</td><td>F000687</td><td>Luke AFB - 56th Fighter Wing</td><td>Title V</td><td>Institutions - Public/Government</td><td>CMP003439</td><td>United States Air Force - Luke AFB</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Alan Thomas, the Air Program Manager for Luke AFB 56th Fighter wing called me to report a malfunction at Storage Tank 368 for unleaded gasoline.</td><td>02/24/2020</td><td>04/02/2020</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>February 24, 2020:

Alan Thomas, the Air Program Manager at Luke AFB 56th Fighter Wing contacted me by phone to report a malfunction at storage tank 368 for unleaded gasoline. Mr. Alan stated, while conducting preventive maintenance on a flow meter, on Tuesday 18 February 2020, the smell of fuel vapors was noticed along with visible vapors, coming from an electrical control panel.
Mr. Alan reported, the system was immediately shutdown and the personnel on site reported the smell and visible vapors ceased immediately. 
Mr. Alan also reported, inspections confirmed no liquid was released from the system. In addition, according to Mr. Alan, further inspections confirmed, a faulty gasket associated with tank level controls allowed the vapors to leak through an electrical panel. 
Mr. Alan said immediate corrective actions were taken by shutting the fueling system down and they are in the process of scheduling repairs to the system before continuing use of this storage tank.

I informed Mr. Alan, I would not issue a notice of noncompliance for this event.
I did ask Mr. Alan to please keep me informed of the repairs to the tank and I stated I may want to be present for some of the repair process.

March 23,2020:

Repairs had been completed on March 18, 2020.
I sent Mr. Alan an e-mail requesting a copy of the repair records for Tank 368.

April 2, 2020:

Mr. Alan sent me the tank repair records as I requested.</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRPT001177</td><td>F002813</td><td>Calgon Carbon Corporation</td><td>Minor</td><td>Chemical Manufacture, Processing, etc</td><td>CMP000654</td><td>Calgon Carbon Corporation</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Permit expires on 31-Oct-2021. A Non-Title V permit renewal application shall be submitted 6 to 18 months prior to the expiration date.

Annual administrative fees received on 25-Sep-2019.

Routine inspection conducted on 12-Apr-2019.

Malfunction notification and report received by Maricopa County Air Quality Department within acceptable time frame.

Stack/emission point: Emissions occurred at the #1 and #2 bypass stack
Date and duration: Event occurred on 26-May-2020, beginning at 1124 hours and ending on or before 1324 hours
Equipment involved: Emissions emanated from #1 kiln to #1 bypass and from #2 kiln to #2 bypass
Nature and cause of malfunction: Brief power outage lasting approximately one minute resulted in a loss of power to all equipment. This opened the bypass stacks stacks after the burners shut off.
Corrective action taken: Source contacted APS to verify no issue was occurring in the area. After verification the equipment was restarted as quickly as possible and brought up to temperature quickly so the bypass stacks could be closed.

Emissions: Per the source the malfunction did not cause the facility to exceed permit limits (calculations provided based on 2016 stack testing).
The calculations assume the duration of the malfunction is from when the stack cap opens to when it is closed and the pollution control system is fully operational or resident time in the reactivation kiln is approximately two hours, thus emissions associated with a malfunction will be limited to a max duration of two hours, whichever is less.

The source is to include malfunction emissions in the rolling total emissions report.

Emissions due to the malfunction event:
NOX – 0.03 lbs 
CO – 6.83 lbs
VOC – 0.01 lbs
PM – 2.55 lbs
HCl – 0.03 lbs</td><td>05/27/2020</td><td>05/28/2020</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Malfunction report</td></tr>
