<tr><td>CRPT001107</td><td>F000055</td><td>Greenfield Water Reclamation Plant</td><td>Minor</td><td>Wastewater Treatment Plants and Lift Stations</td><td>CMP000816</td><td>City of Mesa</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Digester Gas Release Event May 9, 2020.</td><td>05/10/2020</td><td>05/11/2020</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>May 10, 2020: 
Jen Hetherington, the Regulatory Compliance Program Manager for the City of Mesa, Greenfield Water Reclamation Plant, called the Department to report the Greenfield Water Reclamation Plant had a Discharge from the sludge storage tank for about 12 minutes on Saturday, May 9, 2020.
May 11, 2020;
Ms. Hetherington submitted the written report required within 72 hours of the occurrence of the exceedance event. 
According to the report, in preparation for installation of a spectacle blind on the sludge storage tank, the tank had to be opened to the atmosphere while being drained. The tank was isolated by a closed valve, while purging the tank with nitrogen to remove the digester gas, from the tank for 3 days. After 3 days of purging, the pressure relief valve was removed. When the pressure relief valve was removed the methane gas detector sent an alarm notifying the contractor there was still digester gas in the tank. The pressure relief valve was reinstalled to stop the digester gas flow.

Plant personnel determined, the isolation valve did not close completely and prevented the sludge tank gas from purging completely.
Ms. Hetherington reported, “the fence line odor loggers online at the time documented the plant was at all times below 0.03 parts per million by volume maintaining compliance with Condition 5.a of the air permit. Plant operations were delivering gas to the flare and the boilers at the time of the discharge.”
In addition, Ms. Hetherington stated, 21.8 lbs of methane vented to the atmosphere during the 12 minute event.
The twelve month rolling VOC limit is, 40,000 lbs. 

I sent Ms. Hetherington an e-mail to request the fence line H2S monitoring results and records showing the flare was operating on the day of the event.

May 12, 2020:
Ms. Hetherington sent me the information requested, (See Information Request Email_1 and Information Request Email_Response_2).

	
May 26, 2020:
I called Ms. Hetherington to inform her I would not issue a notice of non compliance for this reported event.

.</td></tr>
