<tr><td>CRPT000964</td><td>F000055</td><td>Greenfield Water Reclamation Plant</td><td>Minor</td><td>Wastewater Treatment Plants and Lift Stations</td><td>CMP000816</td><td>City of Mesa</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Self reported deviation for event that occurred on April 9, 2020.</td><td>04/10/2020</td><td>04/30/2020</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>April 10, 2020:
Jen Hetherington, the Regulatory Compliance Program Manager for the City of Mesa, Greenfield Water Reclamation Plant, called the Department to report the Greenfield Water Reclamation Plant had a Discharge of Digester Gas at 10:30 PM on March 12, 2020.
April 13, 2020:
Ms. Hetherington submitted the written report required within 72 hours of the occurrence of the exceedance event. 
According to the report, in preparation for the expansion plan scheduled to take place later in the year, the Mesa staff drained sludge from the sludge storage tank. The sludge drainage allows digester gas to expand and fill the tank. This process involved purging the gas in the digester tank by displacing the gas with water and the gas would be vented to the flares and boilers.  
Ms. Hetherington’s report also explains, a contractor, McCarthy Contracting, was on site conducting work associated with connecting two new digesters to an existing sludge storage tank. Ms. Hetherington stated in the report, “Mesa staff explained to McCarthy that the work should be performed on the right side in part due to concerns that the elevated valve would not close fully. Mesa intended to replace this valve during the digester connection project.”

Ms. Hetherington explains, at approximately, 3:06 PM, the gas vented from the valve opening for approximately twenty minutes. The Plant staff were notified by alarm from the Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition System (“SCADA”) According to Ms. Hetherington’s report, the digester gas (mostly methane) vented through an opening that is not metered so not metered so the flow of gas could not be measured. In addition, there were no alarms indicating high H2S levels. 
Ms. Hetherington also stated, “given the volume of the digester and the pressures at issue, the amount of gas released was relatively modest, and would not significantly affect the City’s compliance with the limitations set forth in Condition 1 of the Permit for Volatile Organic Compounds.” 

April 16, 2020:
I sent Jen Hetherington an e-mail to confirm the boilers and flares were in operation and to ask if she could provide an estimation of the VOC emissions or explain how the determination was made that compliance with the permit’s VOC limits would not be significantly affected.

Ms. Hetherington responded the same day.
Ms. Hetherington said the boilers and one flare were operating at the time of the event.
Ms. Hetherington also provided data to show the H2S and VOC emissions were not exceeded.

April 30, 2020:
I called Ms. Hetherington and informed her I would not issue a notice of noncompliance for this event.
I explained to Ms. Hetherington, Greenfield Water Reclamation Plant, had not exceeded the limits set 
in the Permit for the plant.</td></tr>
