<tr><td>CRPT000923</td><td>F001482</td><td>Ameripride Linen & Apparel Service</td><td>Minor</td><td>Other (Miscellaneous)</td><td>CMP000218</td><td>Ameripride Services Inc</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Per the Chief Engineer Duane Coley at AmeriPride Services 100lb of print towels was accidentally mixed in with an order of shop towels.  The practice of this facility is to remove the shop towels from the truck deliveries and  forward the print towels to a different facility which is permitted for laundering print towels.  Per Mr Coley a new employee inadvertently removed a bag of print towels from the delivery truck along with the bags of shop towels.  The error was not noticed until after the towels had been laundered.

Permit Condition 2.d. states that The Permittee shall not launder any print towels.

Permit Condition 11 states:  Malfunctions, Emergency Upsets, and Excess Emissions: Emissions in excess of an applicable emission limitation shall constitute a violation. However, the Permittee has an affirmative defense in the case of an emergency, malfunction or startup/shutdown if the monitoring, demonstration, recordkeeping, notification and reporting requirements described in Rule 130 and Rule 140 are followed.[Rule 130 §§201, 400][Rule 140 §§400, 500][SIP Rule 140].

Rule 130 does not apply.

Rule 140 requires reporting of emissions in excess of standards and limitations.  The emission standards refer to Rule 510, which does not list a limit for toluene.

Their previous permit allowed for the laundering of print towels and had a permit toluene limit of 2400 lb/yr.  The emission factor used for that permit was 1.44 lb toluene per 1000 lb of print towels laundered.  Laundering 100 lb of print towels therefore would result in an estimated emission of 0.14lb of toluene.  This is not in excess of any County Rule or the Holder's previous permit conditions.  

This report is being taken for reference only.</td><td>03/30/2020</td><td>04/02/2020</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>On 3/30/2020 an email was received listing an accidental laundering of print towels at a commercial laundry facility.</td></tr>
