<tr><td>CRPT000879</td><td>F002813</td><td>Calgon Carbon Corporation</td><td>Minor</td><td>Chemical Manufacture, Processing, etc</td><td>CMP000654</td><td>Calgon Carbon Corporation</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Permit expires on 31-Oct-2021. A Non-Title V permit renewal application shall be submitted 6 to 18 months prior to the expiration date.

Annual administrative fees received on 25-Sep-2019.

Routine inspection conducted on 12-Apr-2019.

Malfunction notification and report received by Maricopa County Air Quality Department within acceptable time frame.

Stack/emission point: #1 bypass stack
Date and duration: 20-Mar-2020, beginning at 1541 hours and ending on or before 1741 hours
Equipment involved: #1 kiln to #1 bypass
Nature and cause of malfunction: The GFCI for the HMI server rack in the MCC tripped out which caused a loss of power to the control computers. Without control of the equipment and communication to it, the equipment began to automatically shut down for safety. The kick out originally was thought to be due to the failed UPS but after unplugging it a general overload of the circuit still occurred. The circuit has too much current draw.
Corrective action taken: Temporary power was run to the plugs for the equipment until a proper power source can be found for the numerous devices that run in the cabinet. This is planned for the next scheduled outage. Feed was off due to interlocks and heat-up of the thermal oxidizer was done quickly to allow the bypass stack to be closed.

Emissions: Per the source the malfunction did not cause the facility to exceed permit limits (calculations provided based on 2016 stack testing).

The calculations assume the duration of the malfunction is from when the stack cap opens to when it is closed and the pollution control system is fully operational or resident time in the reactivation kiln is approximately two hours, thus emissions associated with a malfunction will be limited to a max duration of two hours, whichever is less.

The source is to include malfunction emissions in the rolling total emissions report.

Emissions due to the malfunction event:
NOX – 0.02 pounds
CO – 4.02 pounds
VOC – 0 pounds
PM – 1.50 pounds
HCl – 0.02 pounds

Items used to determine compliance:
Communication, notification, report from source
The Department is pleased to offer a Business Assistance (BA) Program for the regulated community.  The purpose of the BA Program is to provide air quality information and technical assistance to business owners.  They offer permit application assistance, courtesy site visits, rule interpretation and education, and enforcement case review. BA Program may be reached at 602-506-5102 and/or AQbusinessassistance@maricopa.gov.</td><td>03/23/2020</td><td>03/25/2020</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Malfunction report for second incident that occurred on 20-Mar-2020.</td></tr>
