<tr><td>CRPT000876</td><td>F002813</td><td>Calgon Carbon Corporation</td><td>Minor</td><td>Chemical Manufacture, Processing, etc</td><td>CMP000654</td><td>Calgon Carbon Corporation</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Permit expires on 31-Oct-2021. A Non-Title V permit renewal application shall be submitted 6 to 18 months prior to the expiration date.

Annual administrative fees received on 25-Sep-2019.

Routine inspection conducted on 12-Apr-2019.

Malfunction notification and report received by Maricopa County Air Quality Department within acceptable time frame.

Stack/emission point: #1 bypass stack
Date and duration: 20-Mar-2020, beginning at 0909 and ending on or before 1109 hours
Equipment involved: #1 kiln to #1 bypass
Nature and cause of malfunction: A UPS had failed allowing all equipment plugged into it to shut down. The UPS was in the main HMI server rack. When this failed the control room HMI computers lost power and control over the plant. The PLC also lost communication to all VFD's which then shut down as a safety measure.
Corrective action taken: The server equipment was unplugged from the UPS and the process restarted according to standard operating procedures. Feed was off due to interlocks and heat-up of the thermal oxidizer was done quickly to allow the bypass stack to be closed.
Emissions: Per the source the malfunction did not cause the facility to exceed permit limits (calculations provided based on 2016 stack testing).
The calculations assume the duration of the malfunction is from when the stack cap opens to when it is closed and the pollution control system is fully operational or resident time in the reactivation kiln is approximately two hours, thus emissions associated with a malfunction will be limited to a max duration of two hours, whichever is less.

The source is to include malfunction emissions in the rolling total emissions report.

Emissions due to the malfunction event:
NOX – 0.02 lbs
CO – 4.02 lbs
VOC – 0 lbs
PM – 1.50 lbs
HCl – 0.02 lbs

