<tr><td>CRPT000875</td><td>F002813</td><td>Calgon Carbon Corporation</td><td>Minor</td><td>Chemical Manufacture, Processing, etc</td><td>CMP000654</td><td>Calgon Carbon Corporation</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Permit expires on 31-Oct-2021. A Non-Title V permit renewal application shall be submitted 6 to 18 months prior to the expiration date.

Annual administrative fees received on 25-Sep-2019.

Routine inspection conducted on 12-Apr-2019.

Malfunction notification and report received by Maricopa County Air Quality Department within acceptable time frame.

Stack/emission point: #1 bypass stack
Date and duration: 18-Mar-2020, beginning at 1550 and ending on or before 1750 hours
Equipment involved: #1 kiln to #1 bypass
Nature and cause of malfunction: A thunderstorm caused a power outage in the immediate area which stopped the combustion blower and thus the burners. This then opened the bypass stacks as an interlock.
Corrective action taken: Employees waited until the storm passed and then restarted the equipment. Power company notified. Feed was off due to interlocks and heat-up of the thermal oxidizer was done quickly to allow the bypass stack to be closed.
Emissions: Per the source the malfunction did not cause the facility to exceed permit limits (calculations provided based on 2016 stack testing).

The calculations assume the duration of the malfunction is from when the stack cap opens to when it is closed and the pollution control system is fully operational or resident time in the reactivation kiln is approximately two hours, thus emissions associated with a malfunction will be limited to a max duration of two hours, whichever is less.

The source is to include malfunction emissions in the rolling total emissions report.

Emissions due to the malfunction event:
NOX – 0.01 lbs
CO – 2.54 lbs
VOC – 0 lbs
PM – 0.95 lbs
HCl – 0.01 lbs

