<tr><td>CRPT000864</td><td>F000055</td><td>Greenfield Water Reclamation Plant</td><td>Minor</td><td>Wastewater Treatment Plants and Lift Stations</td><td>CMP000816</td><td>City of Mesa</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Exceedance report for event occurring on March 12th involving release of Digester Gas to atmosphere.</td><td>03/13/2020</td><td>03/18/2020</td><td>No</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>March 13, 2020:
Jen Hetherington, the Regulatory Compliance Program Manager for the City of Mesa, Greenfield Water Reclamation Plant, called the Department to report the Greenfield Water Reclamation Plant had a Discharge of Digester Gas at 10:30 PM on March 12, 2020.
March 15, 2020:
Ms. Hetherington submitted the written report required within 72 hours of the occurrence of the exceedance event.
According to the report, on March 12, 2020, digester gas was vented from Digester 1 and SCFM and from the Digested Sludge Storage Tank at a rate of 50 SCFM. The duration of this event according to the report lasted for one hour and 20 minutes.
The following is an explanation from the report explaining the details of this event:
In preparation for upcoming expansion, the Greenfield Water Reclamation Plant (GWRP) installed a Stand Pipe system, an emergency/bypass system for digested sludge to be delivered to the dewatering centrifuges and isolates the Digested Sludge Storage Tank. This was done to prepare future piping connections to be completed on Digester 3 and 4.
At 7:48 PM on the day of the event, the Plant Operator called the Plant Supervisor, Dirk Witter to report the flare and boilers were in alarm status and could not be restarted. 
At 8:02 PM, Mr. Witter arrived at the plant and, according to the report pressure was building but had not started venting to the atmosphere. After Mr. Witter confirmed the boilers and flare were in alarm status, he tried to restart them but was unsuccessful.
At 8:28 PM the gas began venting to the atmosphere to prevent the infrastructure from exploding. Mr. Witter changed valving back to normal operation mode, sending digested sludge to the Digested Sludge Storage Tank and not the Stand Pipe.
At 9:28 PM Once the sludge was completely drained and the drain was closed, the flare and boilers were restarted, and venting of gas ceased at 9:48pm.
According to the report, the stand pipe had been put in use that same day. M. Witter and plant personal found there was a blockage in the methane gas header from the digester complex preventing the flare and boilers from receiving the gas required to operate.
In addition, the report explains:
The Stand Pipe has a level sensor connected to SCADA, this system has alarms to alert the Operator if the sludge level is getting too close to the Condensate Sediment Traps. The report explains, personnel never received the alarm indicating the sludge level was high enough to enter the Condensate Sediment Traps. However; plant personnel were alerted the flare and boilers had stopped operating, initiating the emergency response. Staff personnel believe the level sensor was not reading accurately, and a blockage occurred preventing the flow of gas to the boilers and flares. As a result of findings the Stand Pipe system will not be put in to use again, the level sensor will be repaired.
According to the report fence line levels of H2S never exceeded 0.03ppm of a 30-minute average at the first residence

Greenfield Water Reclamation Plant is claiming Affirmative Response under Rule 140 Section 401.(See attached 72 hour Report)

March 25, 2020: 
I sent Ms. Hetherington an e-mail to ask her if the level sensor for the Stand Pipe has been repaired.
Ms. Hetherington said she would look in to it and let me know.
March 26, 2020:
Ms. Hetherington, sent me an e-mail regarding repairs made to the level sensor for the Stand Pipe system. According to Ms. Hetherington, the sensor has been recalibrated and determined to be functionally correctly. In addition, the sensor alarm level has been lowered to allow for potential errors. Ms. Hetherington also explained the water for the Gas Header will be observed for potential  issues. Furthermore, the dewatering will be increased to keep the stand pipe levels low. Finally, Ms. Hetherington said, the stand pipe has been tested twice and there were no issues and the Plant personnel do not anticipate any issues when the stand pipe is put in operation.</td></tr>
