<tr><td>CRPT000563</td><td>F002504</td><td>Solana Generating Station</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP000333</td><td>Arizona Solar One LLC</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Periodic Reporting</td><td>Semiannual Report all of 2019 and Title V Annual Compliance Report for 2019.</td><td>01/29/2020</td><td>02/10/2020</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Title V Reporting: 
Both Semi-Annuals for 2019 and Annual Certification Statement. This report covers January, 2019 – December 31st, 2019. 
Per permit condition 11:

i.	Emissions Calculations: The Permittee shall include the results of all the required rolling 12-month emissions calculations for each month in the six-month reporting period. 

Listed in records attachment table 1. All required information on table, there were no emissions that were out of permitted limits. Emergency generators ran for 78.7 & 77.0 hours for emergency downtime (plant shutdown). 

ii.	Deviation Reporting: The Permittee shall identify all instances of deviations from permit requirements in the semi-annual monitoring report.  The Permittee shall include the probable cause of such deviations, and any corrective actions or preventative measures taken.

CEMS certification and ullage performance testing due between June 1st, - August 31, 2020

iii.	CEMS Reporting: The Permittee shall furnish the Control Officer a copy of the written report of the results of the CEMS performance evaluation.

CEMS certification and ullage performance testing due between June 1st, - August 31, 2020

iv.	LDAR Plan Reporting: The Permittee shall furnish the following summary information for each monitoring period.

1)	The number of valves, pumps, flanges/connectors, ball joints, pressure relief valves and any U/L joints for which leaks were detected, the percent leakers, and the total number of valves, pumps, flanges/connectors, ball joints, pressure relief valves and any U/L joints monitored;

For the full year of 2019, 3305 ball joints were registered and graded based off LDAR scale. 2,989 ball joint were “repacked”.

2)	The number of valves, pumps, flanges/connectors, ball joints, pressure relief valves and any U/L joints for which leaks were not repaired, identifying the number of those that are determined nonrepairable;

Facility lists 316 ball joints scheduled to be repacked at this point (less than 2% of ball joint total) 0 ball joints are listed as nonrepairable. See page 2 chart inside records submitted for more details.

3)	The facts that explain any delay of repairs and, where appropriate, why a process unit/collector loop shutdown was technically infeasible;

The facility states:

“Ball Joints are located in operable loops containing at leas 20 ball joints or 40 ball joints, if a double-loop. It is contractually infeasible to shut down each loop where a ball joint is identified leaking due to the APS energy provision agreement and Department of Energy contract. We track each leaking ball joint by emission grade, as identified in the LDAR. Loops are prioritized for shut down and repair based on the number of ball joints leaking and severity of the leaks. Note that nay Grade 1 leak is immediately isolated and that loop is taken out of service until a repacking or repair can be completed.”

4)	The results of all monitoring conducted within the semiannual reporting period.
[County Rule 210 §§ 302.1(e)(1) and 305.1(c)(1)]
See table inside semi-annual record submittal. 

The permittee has satisfied the semiannual report and annual compliance certification report requirements per permit condition 11. There were no compliance issues observed amongst these reports.</td></tr>
