<tr><td>CRPT000431</td><td>F001759</td><td>Gila River Power Station</td><td>Title V</td><td>Power Plant</td><td>CMP015964</td><td>Salt River Project</td><td>Compliance Report</td><td>Self-Report</td><td>Notification Date: January 13, 2020</td><td>01/14/2020</td><td>01/14/2020</td><td>Yes</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>January 13, 2020:
In Violation of the following:
1.240 § 305.1: In addition to emission limits expressed elsewhere in this Permit, the Permittee shall not cause, allow, or permit emissions to exceed the limits shown in Tables 4

2.140 § 501.1: Notification by email, telephone or facsimile within 24 hours of the time when the Permittee first learned of the occurrence of excess emissions.


At approximately, 1:00 PM, I received an e-mail from Roy Campbell of Gila River Power Station (GRPS), reporting excess carbon monoxide (CO) start up emissions on Unit 1B, on January 11, 2020.
Mr. Campbell informed me in his e-mail, this incident was under investigation and he would send a report to me, once they know what caused this exceedance.
The startup/shut down emissions limit for CO is 212 lb/hr, one hour average per Permit Condition 18. A. Table 4 Startup Emissions. According to the initial e-mail, the magnitude of emissions were as follows:
1/11/2020 – 231.9 CO at 0200.

January 14, 2020:
Mr. Campbell submitted the Excess Emissions Report. According to the report, the exceedance occurred due to operational error because the operator on duty was distracted by problems with other pant equipment during start up. In addition, the report explains, an Operations Specialist Trainee was performing the startup operation and did not have sufficient oversight by the trainer at the time of exceedance.

Startup Emissions Exceedance:
Identity of Emission Point: Unit 1B
Magnitude of Emission: 231.9 lb/hr, CO Startup Limit
Permit Limitation: 212 lb/hr, CO Startup Limit (1-hour average)
Duration of Emission: 1 hour
Actual Emissions: 231.9 lb/hr on 1/11/2020 at 0200 hrs.

Corrective actions described in the report involved training the Operations Specialists to “identify
both the conditions leading to the excess emissions and possible ways to avoid that should it ever arise in the future.” 
Alarms for the CEMS system to provide a unique tone to alert the operators to possible excess emissions, are being investigated. 
In addition, “Operations Specialists have been instructed to reinforce plant startup requirements to Automatic Generation Control dispatchers.”

January 15, 2020:
I called Mr. Campbell to discuss the excess emissions reported. I left a message for Mr. Campbell to call me.
January 16, 2020:
I spoke to Mr. Campbell and I informed him I would issue a Notice of Non Compliance for exceeding the allowable emission limit for Startup CO per Permit Condition 18.A. Table 4.
In addition, I informed Mr. Campbell, I am issuing a Notice of Non Compliance for failure to report the exceedance within 24 hours of occurrence.

Compliance determined by:
Records provided by the source
Statements made by the source</td></tr>
